WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:10.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Apologies for absence: Wendy
Becker and Munir Pirmohamed can’t make it

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:16.390
today. And any declarations of interest that
aren’t already declared? No.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:21.570
Well, I’m going to firstly welcome Julian
Kelly who’s joined us; this is your first

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:29.210
Board meeting, so welcome to the NHS, Julian.
And to thank Matt very much for standing in

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:34.120
brilliantly for the last sort of six months
– is it six months? Yeah, so thank you,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.200
Matt. You’ve done a great job, so thank
you very much.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.350
And Dido, you’ve got a couple of goodbyes
to do, as well.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.681
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes, you do the hellos, I
do the goodbyes. So, unfortunately, she hasn’t

00:00:44.681 --> 00:00:49.230
been able to be with us, but this is Glynis
Breakwell’s last Board meeting as a non-exec,

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:54.370
so we wish her well, and we have a small thank-you
gift and a card that’s circulating, so that

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:00.300
we can get that to her. It’s also the last
public Board meeting for Stephen Hay and for

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.330
Kathy McLean, who will be not leaving the
organisation immediately, but reasonably soon,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:11.760
and I think we should mark both of you, not
just your contribution to the boards of NHS

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.000
Improvement, but to the NHS as a whole. You
will be sorely missed when we actually let

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.130
you go, but thank you very, very much, and
I wanted to put on the record, I’m sure

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.920
the rest of the Board want to join me in thanking
you, and wishing you the very best in whatever

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.300
you choose to do next.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good, thank you. Minutes of

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.210
the previous meeting; everyone happy with
them? Any questions? And there are no matters

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:42.750
arising. So, we can approve the minutes.
My report: there’s one bit of formal business,

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:48.850
which is to approve the recommendation from
ARAC to approve the amendments to the standing

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.640
orders and scheme of delegation ahead of the
new operating model from April 1st 2019, and

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.540
I think that’s just a standard thing to
do. Simon, you’ve been through it already,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.490
and so can we just approve that? Excellent.
So, on my report, just a couple of things

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:12.870
I was going to mention. I’ve been to three
new integrated care systems: Cumbria, South

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:18.889
Tyneside and Surrey. And all three are, you
know, doing some really good things. But what

00:02:18.889 --> 00:02:24.030
really struck me, the basis of all of them
was the relationships on the ground. If there

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:29.010
are not good relationships, whatever we do
centrally, whatever changes to the law we

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:34.200
do, it’s just not going to work; there has
got to be those sort of trusting, good relationships.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.120
In those three areas, working really well.
And the other thing I’d say is, because

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:45.079
the CSUs haven’t always had a good press,
but I went to the northern CSU, NECS, and

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:52.100
it was just fantastic. And we saw at a meeting
earlier today some of the population health

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.720
statistics and methodologies that are being
developed, and I thought it was really, really

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.260
first class, and Matthew and Emily, you’ve
both been very much involved in that; I thought

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.299
it was really fantastic.
That was all I was going to say, but Dido,

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.100
do you have a – did you want to say anything
before going to Simon?

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.200
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I only had a couple of small
things to say. The first one was just, on

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:18.370
my travels in the last month, I have been
more workforce, people plan-focused. I’ve

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:26.260
spoken and attended the CNO summit. Been to
a number of NHS provider meetings with a large

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:33.519
number of chairs and chief executives. Our
own BME network gathering in Euston that,

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:38.980
David, you and I were at. And actually, yesterday,
I was at a meeting with the armed forces looking

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:45.150
at how we encourage more reservists in the
NHS. And the reason I share all of those is,

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:49.030
there’s an overwhelming theme – and Julian
Hartley will talk a lot more about it – that

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:54.720
is coming from everyone we talk to about how
important it is to make the NHS a better place

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.779
for people to work, that everyone I talk to
is proud to work in the NHS, but would like

00:03:59.779 --> 00:04:06.540
us to be a better employer. And a better employer
that encourages diversity, flexibility, and

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.549
a modern, 21st-century way of working. And
in each of those interactions I’ve had,

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:17.310
it’s been exactly the same thing, so that’s
been the theme of my month since the last

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.990
public Board meeting. And very strong feedback
from everyone out and about that that means

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:29.050
that we all need to change too, that the – as
we mark, you know, next week, the coming together

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:34.300
– and I’m sure Simon will mention it – the
formal beginning of our full joint endeavour

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:41.180
between NHS England and NHS Improvement, how
we use that as a moment to start to kickstart

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.599
our own culture change. So, that’s my big
theme.

00:04:44.599 --> 00:04:50.729
The other piece, just to put on the record,
David, we had our second delivery, quality

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:58.520
and performance committee this morning for
review of the operational quality and financial

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:03.800
performance of the NHS as it stands today.
We spent quite a long time this morning talking

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:09.960
about how we develop the implementation plans
for the long-term plan, a review in the detail

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.400
on the workforce planning and our own business
plan for NHS England and NHS Improvement,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.740
and we’ll publish the minutes in due course,
but just wanted to put that on the record.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.020
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, thanks, Dido. Simon?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you. Well, first of

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:30.710
all, just picking up where Dido left off.
As she says, Monday marks the first of three

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:36.960
sets of big changes across NHS England and
NHS Improvement to bring the organisations

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.620
together. A number of teams will be coming
into new alignment as of the 1st of April.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:50.640
Then secondly, there will be a further set
of changes around the summertime, and then

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.280
thirdly, completing that in the autumn. So,
I just want to thank everybody across both

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:03.710
NHS Improvement and NHS England who is going
through this process. We will be making important

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:08.400
steps as of Monday the 1st of April, but I
think we all recognise – the boards recognise

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.960
– that this is actually a process that is
going to be taking six months or more, and

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.819
we’re immensely grateful for the ongoing
support of all of our colleagues as we do

00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:30.730
that, showing in the combining of a number
of these roles over the next several months.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:35.920
Secondly, to say that it won’t have escaped
your notice that tomorrow is, of course, Friday,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:44.300
March 29th. We are being asked by the government
now not to assume that Friday is the date

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:52.979
on which we should entertain the no deal contingency,
but that has now moved to April 12th. It’s

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:58.569
not impossible that that date may move again,
but the message both to people across the

00:06:58.569 --> 00:07:03.129
NHS and inside NHS England and NHS Improvement,
again, is an enormous thank you for all of

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:08.360
the work that’s gone into contingency planning.
Keith Willett and team: a lot of work across

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.530
the Health Service. We’re not out of the
woods yet, so we’re asking people to stay

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:20.580
focused on that possibility, with April 12th
as the next nearest date.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:29.320
Thirdly, we are, as we discussed this morning,
in the final stages of the ’19/20 operational

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:34.319
planning and contracting round. And I think
it’s fair to say that an enormous amount

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:39.290
of progress has been made, even in the last
fortnight, but there is a lot to get done

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:45.800
between now and Easter, both in terms of syncing
up the financial, the efficiency, the workforce

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:53.780
and the activity plans, so that we collectively
go into the year with a viable set of operating

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:59.800
plans across systems and institutions. And
our new regional teams are going to be working

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:05.870
very closely with system leaders on that.
We’re aiming to have a coherent total view

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:12.349
for the NHS either by Easter or very shortly
thereafter, so, again, just for the Board

00:08:12.349 --> 00:08:19.870
to note, as we discussed this morning.
And then lastly, there is, in parallel with

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:26.020
this, an enormous amount of work going on
now to co-design the long-term plan implementation

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:34.570
framework, continuing the inclusive process
we used in the LTP production itself and the

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:38.900
approach that has been taken in the workforce
initial report, and I know Dido you’re going

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:45.420
to be talking about that later on with Julian
this afternoon. But in addition to that, we

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:51.310
are today identifying the founding members
of the new NHS Assembly to be part of guiding

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:59.730
coalition in this inclusive way for LTP implementation.
We are also on Tuesday afternoon going to

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:04.870
be discussing with parliament further details
of the legislative proposals that were set

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:13.470
out in the long-term plan, and then we aim
in late April or May to publish the LTP implementation

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:18.060
framework which will then be working collectively
across the Health Service and with our partners

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.340
between now and the autumn to set out what
that five-year programme of change looks like

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.560
in every part of the country.
So, those will be the four things, really.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.030
Thanks to people inside the organisation.
A reminder as to where we stand on Brexit.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:35.880
The ‘19/20 operating plans. And the LTP
implementation. I think the rest we’re going

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.040
to be discussing during the course of the
agenda.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.220
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Simon. And Ian, have
you got anything?

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.680
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Just one item, then, in that
context. I think just for the record, worth

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:52.870
reporting that the formal merger of South
Tyneside NHS Foundation Trust and City Hospital

00:09:52.870 --> 00:10:00.120
Sunderland NHS Foundation Trust has moved
a step closer. Recommend both boards have

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.890
applied to come together from the 1st of April,
and acting as Chief Executive of Monitor,

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:11.870
I have signed the dissolution order that dissolves
those two trusts, and they then come together

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:19.720
as the South Tyneside and Sunderland NHS Foundation
Trust from the 1st of the 4th 2019. A combined

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:26.151
workforce of 8,000 staff serving over 800,000
people across the North East of England, so

00:10:26.151 --> 00:10:32.030
that’s something for the record, I think.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Any questions for

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:42.280
Simon and/or Ian? Anyone like to raise anything?
No? Okay, we’ll crack straight on, then.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:46.300
I think Pauline, you’re going to give us
an update on – where are you? Oh, there

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:52.250
you are, Pauline. You’re a long way away.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes, so the Board, you have

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:59.350
in front of you a report detailing performance
from February, but I am going to bring you

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:07.110
up to date. Clearly, some of the references
that I make will be based on unpublished data,

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:13.120
but it’ll give you a sense of direction
of where we are at the moment.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:19.390
I would like to start by thanking all of our
staff throughout the NHS for the work that

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:25.130
they have done during the winter period. All
of our clinical staff, all of our managerial

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:30.650
staff, people working at a local level, a
regional level, and, indeed, a national level.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:37.320
And I think it’s fair to say at this point
that when the story of this winter is written,

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:42.970
it’ll demonstrate that the work that has
been put in over the course of the last 12

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:49.850
months on the transformation front has really
prepared us well for the winter period, and

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:56.830
I think it will be the case that our performance
this winter was better than last winter, and

00:11:56.830 --> 00:12:02.050
that’s probably about the first time in
five years that that has happened. But it’s

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:08.780
very much down to the commitment, dedication,
of all – everybody who really works within

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.520
the NHS.
At the moment, we are aware that we have seen

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:21.650
within four hours about 380,000 more patients
than during the same period last year, and

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:28.280
that’s against a background of having some
increase in ambulance arrivals, some increase

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:34.410
in attendances, and, indeed, in emergency
admissions. But on the emergency admission

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:39.570
front, I would also just like to draw people’s
attention to why some of that is happening,

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:46.220
and, again, it’s around our transformation
work, the increase in same-day emergency care

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:52.200
and those attendances, which we’re very
pleased about, because it’s avoiding people

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:59.810
having to stay in hospital overnight, but
it’s still coded in the data as normal admissions.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:05.390
When we look at our transformation work, we’ve
seen an ongoing increase in the number of

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:14.090
people telephoning 111 and using 111 online.
The percentage for the winter period will

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:22.550
be somewhere in the relation of about 12%,
and over 50% of those people who’ve contacted

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:30.510
111 will have received clinical input during
the course of that call.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:36.070
Last year, we were very concerned about the
amount of time some of our ambulance handovers

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:42.640
were taking, when patients arrived at hospital.
This winter, we have seen a very different

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:49.910
picture. We have got a small number of organisations
that continue to have problems, but overall,

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:55.220
the amount of time people taking – from
the time they arrive at a hospital to being

00:13:55.220 --> 00:14:01.360
brought into the emergency department, etc.,
has improved enormously, and when we publish

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.070
our data, we’ll be able to give more information
on that.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:10.230
As far as patients who are having long waits
within the emergency department is concerned,

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:17.420
we’ve seen a 30% decrease over the period.
Again, because of the transformation work

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:23.670
that we have done during the year, we have
seen the results around the length of time

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:29.930
people are spending in hospital. For seven
days – our percentage of people who are

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:37.410
staying more than seven days, we’ve had
an improvement of 7%. 21 day plus: 11%. And

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:44.860
an ongoing improvement on our DTOCs metric.
And during the last four months, we have seen

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:50.550
a decrease in the number of operations that
are cancelled.

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:57.890
We talked at our previous meeting about the
impact that flu was having this winter, and

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:05.060
I think we discussed at that time the fact
that the vaccine that we were using, the trivalent

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:13.550
vaccine, was a very good match for the virus,
H1N1. And that the type – the strain of

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:22.320
the virus was a strain that was mainly affecting
adults and younger people. But I think when

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:28.930
we have reviewed all of our data for the period,
it’s important to note that the impact on

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:35.810
our hospitals was still quite significant
during the whole of the winter period: the

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:42.350
use of ITU, HTU beds was about the same as
last winter, and during February, the use

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:47.480
of all of our general adult beds was actually
higher than the previous February. So, it

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:54.090
wasn’t a case of the NHS did better as far
as emergency care is concerned because flu

00:15:54.090 --> 00:16:02.040
wasn’t the same burden. It was – well,
it was much closer to last year than we have

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:06.700
recognised, but I think what happened was
that the hospital and the whole system coped

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:11.480
better.
As far as vaccination levels are concerned,

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:17.450
one of the things that we worked on earlier
in the year was to try and increase the uptake

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:25.510
as far as staff are concerned, and there again,
we saw a better picture: 70.3% of our staff

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:31.130
were vaccinated this winter against 68.7%
the previous winter.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:39.990
So, I think all told, the picture across emergency
care demonstrates that the work that’s been

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:46.450
done on transformation is really starting
to pay dividends. As far as being out there

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:51.630
in our emergency departments, at the front
of our hospitals, staff are reporting and

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:57.950
we are seeing far less congestion than in
the previous year. And clearly, from this

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:05.010
point onwards, on an ongoing basis, our focus
needs to continue to be on transformation.

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:10.770
Within the long-term plan, the operating plan,
etc., we have set out the importance of having

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:17.370
even more same-day emergency care, and really
doing better as far as length of stay is concerned.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:23.270
We freed up about 2,000 beds for this winter.
We want to be able to do more for next winter.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:29.690
I’ll just move on to say a little bit about
elective care at the same time. Again, we

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:36.250
don’t have any end-of-year data available,
but I think from the information that has

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:43.770
been available to us up until the end of February,
the more recent service information, we feel

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:49.540
that we have managed to keep the waiting list
stable during the winter period, and that

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:54.350
our previous predictions about the growth
of the waiting list, that we have been able

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:59.110
to do better than we had predicted earlier
in the year. And again, we’ll have that

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:05.170
data available next – well, next month,
next week, actually.

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:11.850
As far as 52 weeks – i.e., patients who
have long waits – we are working very hard

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:16.810
to deliver against the mandate, and there
was a 52% reduction and we’re seeing good

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:25.670
progress. As part of our planning round, we’re
working across the service looking at the

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:33.600
capacity that we need to deliver, and how
we can best return to a situation whereby

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:40.270
our waiting list is akin to the March ’18
number. So, there’s a determination to do

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:48.710
that. And as in emergency care, really focusing
on the transformation areas, managing demand,

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:52.510
etc.
So, I think that’s a very quick update from

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:53.650
me. Happy to take questions.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Before we ask questions –

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:59.460
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Matthew’s doing the rest.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes. Shall I pick up

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:06.090
on the other areas other than elective and
emergency? Thank you. Very good. So, just

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:11.870
touching on some highlights from the report,
I think it’s – firstly, linking to Pauline’s

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:16.260
access point, it’s worth noting that the
NHS app is now rolled out across the country

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:24.350
for accessing 111 and nhs.uk, and we have
about 20% of GP practices now connected to

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:29.040
the app. There’s going to be a big push
in the April to July period to get more GP

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:33.740
practices connected, and then an expectation
we will run a big publicity campaign around

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:39.110
September next year in order to ensure that
it’s in place to support running into next

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:47.760
winter. And worth noting that of the 1.4 million
calls that 111 took in February, about 10%

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:52.890
of them were through online contact rather
than through direct telephone contact, so

00:19:52.890 --> 00:20:00.660
we’re beginning to make that move, which
I think’s very good news.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:07.110
On cancer, we have started the roll-out of
the first long-term plan action in the deployment

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:14.410
of pilots for the lung health checks. This
is going to be a £70 million programme which

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:21.309
we have kicked off the pilots in Manchester
and Liverpool, and will roll out to the ten

00:20:21.309 --> 00:20:30.730
areas of highest incidence of lung cancer,
covering about 600,000 people as time goes

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:38.900
forward. We are still struggling to hit the
62-day target, but I think it is worth noting

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:46.730
that we have now this year seen a – whilst
we’ve seen a growth in the number of patients

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:54.730
seen, referred and seen, by 16%, and a growth
of 10.5% in the actual number of cancer treatments

00:20:54.730 --> 00:21:00.870
coming through the 62-day pathway – so the
– what we’re seeing is very large increases

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:06.630
in people accessing the service, the NHS gearing
up its services very significantly, but not

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:11.480
by as large a number as the demand is going
up by. So, as we’re running into the planning

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:15.290
round that Simon’s just referred to, there’s
a lot of work going on to make sure that we

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:21.940
have the capacity in place across the NHS
to absorb these double-digit growths in demand,

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:28.870
because if we’re going to fulfil the long-term
plan ambition of improving cancer survival

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.750
rates, we do need more people to come forward.
We do – this is good news, that we’re

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:37.380
seeing this demand, and it’s up to the NHS
to respond to the challenge. In urology, we’ve

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:47.990
seen an over 25% increase in access of urology
cancer services, and we have treated 7,500

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:54.640
more people this year.
With primary care, we now have 100% coverage

00:21:54.640 --> 00:22:02.470
of extended access across evenings and weekends
across the country. And we are – we’ve

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:08.820
made really good progress in recruiting additional
staff into primary care; we have exceeded

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:14.850
the 5,000 target of additional staff. In terms
of actual GPs, we have – we were about flat

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:20.300
on the whole-time equivalent number of GPs
over the last year, which is not taking us

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:28.080
to the 5,000 growth in GPs we wanted. Interestingly,
we’ve seen a – in the past year an increase

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:35.150
of about 500 in the head count in GPs, but
because there’s a change in working practice

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:41.820
of GPs, the actual total number of hours has
stayed about constant. Our overseas recruitment

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:48.310
programme in Europe has now recruited 110
overseas doctors, of which 50 are now in country

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:57.770
and working in practices, and we have a continued
pipeline for those. And we’ve had 780 applications

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:03.130
for our improved induction and refresher scheme
of doctors trained in England, possibly in

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:06.770
the UK or maybe overseas, wanting to come
back and practise in the NHS and wanting to

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:12.380
go through that programme, so we think that
is a – that’s very positive.

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:18.500
In mental health, we’re on track or are
hitting all of the standards laid out in the

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:25.450
Five Year Forward View. And we are expecting
every CCG to meet its obligation under the

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:32.550
mental health investment standard this year,
when this year comes in to land. So, that’s

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:37.480
a significant, I think, achievement across
the country.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:44.590
On learning disabilities, we will miss the
target for the end of March. We set ourselves

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:50.670
an ambition in March 2015 to reduce the number
of children and adults with learning disabilities

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:58.970
who are in inpatient facilities from 2,890
to 1,800. We’re going to end the year at

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:05.200
around 2,200, so we will have reduced the
number of learning disabilities patients in

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:11.809
hospital by around 700, but we’ll be around
400 short. Now, that’s not through a lack

00:24:11.809 --> 00:24:20.010
of effort across the country, and in London,
we are within the target range for the appropriate

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:24.750
number of inpatients for the population, and
in the South East and South West, we’re

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:30.380
very close to the target range. But in the
Midlands and the North, both areas are about

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:36.390
200 more patients in beds than we would want
to see. So, there’s a lot of focus now going

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:41.260
on, on, ‘Okay, so if working really hard
to improve this situation hasn’t got us

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:46.120
all the way there, what do we have to do differently
next year and the year after to achieve this

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:51.800
target’, and we are looking at the work
to make sure that more people get care and

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:56.630
treatment, or children, Care, Education and
Treatment Reviews, before a decision to admit

00:24:56.630 --> 00:25:01.840
them is put in place, because we know that
if you do a proper review of a child or an

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:06.900
adult before you admit them to hospital, the
chances of you being able to design a community-based

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:11.390
service for them is much higher than if you
do it after they’ve been admitted. So, there’s

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:16.559
a lot of work going on around that and a lot
of work going on in partnership with local

00:25:16.559 --> 00:25:20.790
authorities to say, ‘Are we commissioning
the right capacity, the right volumes and

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:28.990
the right sorts of care to find safe and appropriate
and supportive ways of looking after and supporting

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:35.080
patients that doesn’t mean admitting patients?’
So, this is still a very high priority for

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:43.200
us, and looking specifically at children with
learning disabilities in hospital.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:50.630
The count at the moment when we started this
was about 170 children in hospital with learning

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:57.210
disabilities. It’s now at about 250. Now,
there’s a couple of things going on simultaneously

00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:01.680
here. One is, we’ve turned to focus on it,
and we are now identifying more children,

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:08.780
so the baseline is increasing. And that’s
a good thing, because we can’t help these

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:12.120
kids unless we know who they are and where
they are. There’s also a bit of reclassification,

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:17.179
because over the same time period, the number
of children in tier-four mental health beds

00:26:17.179 --> 00:26:22.500
fell by about 100, so I think these children
are being more appropriately allocated to

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:27.780
the right sort of support. But there is also
a growing identification of children who need

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:32.809
learning disabilities support or autism support
in schools, and we need to make sure that

00:26:32.809 --> 00:26:37.300
our services are in place, that the default
isn’t for them to be admitted to hospital,

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:43.720
but the default is for them to be cared for
in their own homes or in appropriate schools,

00:26:43.720 --> 00:26:49.090
and that’s – I think that’s a big challenge
for the NHS going forward, is to change our

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:55.360
mindset, and as we identify more people with
more demand, that we provide the right services

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:58.160
and we don’t make the mistake of trying
to hit the target and miss the point, because

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:01.780
this is actually – in my mind, this is a
human rights issue. This is actually about

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:06.840
delivering the appropriate care to some of
our most vulnerable members of the population,

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:10.300
and there’s a real opportunity to transform
lives here if we get it right. So, it’s

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:17.510
not just about the numbers, it’s about,
what are we doing for this cohort of patients?

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:23.100
Then just the last thing I would like to touch
on is just around the work that’s going

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:28.450
on now around this transformation, creating
– with STPs, moving across the integrated

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:33.799
care systems, and there’s a huge amount
of work now going on supporting regions, and

00:27:33.799 --> 00:27:40.830
regions supporting STPs in the development
of primary care networks and creating the

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:44.590
work that I think we’re beginning to see
come through in the planning round for next

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:49.270
year of communities coming together and thinking
about population health needs and working

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:57.170
much more as a system in order to drive things
– to drive forward better care, and that

00:27:57.170 --> 00:28:01.220
work, I think, is making very significant
progress now. Thank you, David.

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:05.940
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Pauline and Matthew.
Any questions for – can – I’ve got two

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:10.150
questions. One is, I don’t know whether
maybe Steve may be able to answer this question

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:15.840
as much as you, but the five-year survival
rate for breast cancer, there’s – some

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:20.429
figures came out during the month, didn’t
they, showing that we’d improved very considerably.

00:28:20.429 --> 00:28:25.309
Is – do you have – can you – where do
those come – where do they come from, those

00:28:25.309 --> 00:28:29.970
figures? And how valid are they? Do you know?
I mean –

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:33.340
&gt;&gt;Stephen Hay: Can you remember the source,
because I’m just trying to –

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:36.980
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes, so it’s a European
study, it was published last week, and it

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:42.980
showed an 18% fall in the deaths from breast
cancer over the last five years, which means

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:50.130
we are now at par with European breast cancer
death rates, and it’s the fastest rate of

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:54.890
improvement in any of six major European countries.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay then. And I had a second

00:28:54.890 --> 00:29:02.490
question. On the people with learning difficulties,
what is the irreducible minimum? I mean, what

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:09.721
is the – in terms of how many people ought
to be cared for in a hospital setting? Do

00:29:09.721 --> 00:29:13.360
we have a view on that?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, we have a view.

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:21.980
There is a range per – of what is felt to
be likely to be the appropriate number of

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:28.530
inpatients per million population. And so
we track each part of the country against

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:33.160
that, which is why we know that London, the
South East and the South West are at the top

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:38.360
end of that range, all of them, but they’re
broadly speaking at or near to that range,

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:47.390
whereas Midlands and North is much higher,
and so there are – this is not an ambition

00:29:47.390 --> 00:29:51.240
to remove inpatient care altogether. There
are some people for whom this is the appropriate

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:56.880
care. But at the moment, we have more patients
in inpatient care than is appropriate. But

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:00.380
yes, there are clinical guidelines on what
you should – where you should expect to

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:01.971
be.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: If you can sort of put it – make

00:30:01.971 --> 00:30:05.150
it part of the operations report, maybe, on
a six-month sort of biannual basis, or something,

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:08.220
and track that?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, it’s part of the

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:12.050
reporting that we do.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks. Yeah, Joanne.

00:30:12.050 --> 00:30:16.040
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Matthew, I’ve got a question
about the NHS app. So, it’s great to see

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:21.130
that rolled out and available. I’ve had
a bit of a mixed experience. Mostly very,

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:25.730
very positive. So, I was a bit surprised – I’ve
got an NHS logon now. I was a bit surprised

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:32.360
at how sort of multi-stage and complex that
was, and having to photograph my driving licence

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:39.000
and speak a video. But that – it worked
seamlessly, and I got my logon almost instantly.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:43.990
So, you know, that was great.
When I got into my – so my practice is linked

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:49.120
up and I can make an appointment. When I got
to that stage, trying to make an appointment,

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:55.290
I was a bit baffled by the terminology, so
the way that they had categorised appointments

00:30:55.290 --> 00:31:00.480
maybe, and so on. I guess that’s very normal,
you know, first iteration of an app.

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:03.299
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: But if I was a bit stumped,

00:31:03.299 --> 00:31:07.429
then I imagine that’s probably going to
be quite a common experience. So, my question

00:31:07.429 --> 00:31:13.429
was, what’s the – what are your plans
for gathering user feedback and progressively

00:31:13.429 --> 00:31:18.380
iterating and improving the user experience?
Because it’s obviously so powerful.

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:25.580
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah, they’re both
very good questions. So, on the login, that

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:30.340
is a best-of-breed experience for doing it.
As in, previous to that, you had to actually

00:31:30.340 --> 00:31:36.490
physically go to your GP surgery. And so,
that – we are the first adopters of that

00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:41.350
as a, do a video, talk into it, log in. At
the moment, it can take up to half an hour

00:31:41.350 --> 00:31:46.820
to do, which is less time than it takes to
get on a bus and go to your GP surgery, but

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:52.000
it’s still too long. We are expecting and
are piloting internally at the moment mechanisms

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:55.320
that will take that down to almost real time.
So –

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:56.320
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Which is my experience.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: So, there’s work going

00:31:56.320 --> 00:32:02.030
on, on that. We are also introducing at the
moment a biometric, so facial recognition

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:05.150
and thumbprint, so you don’t have to put
your password in every time, in response to

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:12.470
user feedback, because there is a continuous
process of user feedback. The – what happens

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:17.920
when you drop into a GP application is obviously
one of the biggest challenges, because they’ve

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:22.309
tended to have existed however they’ve been
defined locally, and they haven’t been set

00:32:22.309 --> 00:32:29.400
up for patients to book initially; they’ve
been set up so that receptionists and doctors

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:32.670
and nurses know perfectly well what they mean,
and moving that thing to a language which

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:38.090
is transparent to an ordinary user is – in
some ways isn’t an app problem, it’s a,

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:42.400
how do we get a standardisation of a language
that the public can use. But there’s a continuous

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:47.440
iteration of consumer feedback around that.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: And how does a consumer provide

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:49.679
that feedback, Matthew?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think that there’s

00:32:49.679 --> 00:32:55.429
a series of – we have our areas that were
in the original pilots where we are continuing

00:32:55.429 --> 00:32:59.540
to work with, so places like Liverpool and
Hastings. There’s a number of those around

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:03.730
the country. And then there’s focus groups,
and then I think you can respond through the

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:04.890
app to make comments.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you.

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:07.330
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, I’ve got two questions
if I may, David; one for Pauline and one for

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:17.740
Matthew. The one for Pauline is, so, first
of all, tribute to the NHS team, in all its

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:21.990
sense, for the sterling work over winter and
what’s been delivered, and to you and your

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:29.120
team for your work with each of the individual
trusts and other parts of the system. I’m

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:33.830
really intrigued, however, by what happened
in February, and maybe it’s unfair to ask

00:33:33.830 --> 00:33:44.900
now, but do we understand why we’re seeing
a greater admission in February compared to

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:52.760
what we were expecting, given that generally,
flu was less aggressive than we might have

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:57.470
expected, and what that tells us for going
forward? What’s the learning? It’s probably

00:33:57.470 --> 00:34:01.780
unfair given it’s only March.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yeah. So, as I mentioned

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:06.440
earlier, David, we’re doing a piece of work,
and I should be able to report on it probably

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:12.309
about the time of the next meeting. And it’s
to look at the overall number of attendances

00:34:12.309 --> 00:34:16.999
and the number of admissions during this whole
of the winter period, because I think what

00:34:16.999 --> 00:34:22.399
we recognise is that that data is quite contaminated
by the transformation that we’ve put into

00:34:22.399 --> 00:34:28.549
the system. So, for example, on admissions,
that we are counting, as I mentioned earlier,

00:34:28.549 --> 00:34:35.239
same-day emergency care as ordinary admissions,
and clearly, we’re trying to drive up the

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:39.919
amount of same-day emergency care in the system,
and, you know, I think I said at a previous

00:34:39.919 --> 00:34:44.339
meeting, we’ve had a little explosion of
it in the last 12 months; it’s gone up by

00:34:44.339 --> 00:34:50.669
about 12%, and increasing as the days go by,
but all being coded as admission activity.

00:34:50.669 --> 00:34:56.899
And we’ve seen something similar happening
on the attendance side, which, it becomes

00:34:56.899 --> 00:35:00.940
more and more interesting the more and more
you delve into it and the more data that becomes

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:07.710
available, because we can see that some of
that increase is because of the new pathways

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:13.700
of care that we’ve introduced around type-three
pathways, urgent treatment centres, collocating

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:18.640
them on the site of a hospital, etc. But I
think we do recognise that underpinning all

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:24.369
of this for the winter period, we’ve had
some increase in attendances and demand, but

00:35:24.369 --> 00:35:29.359
we’ll be able to unbundle all of that over
the course of the next four to six weeks as

00:35:29.359 --> 00:35:33.579
we get our sus data, etc., in.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: My second question to Matthew

00:35:33.579 --> 00:35:42.319
is, clearly, NHS 111 played a material part
of coping with demand, which is really great,

00:35:42.319 --> 00:35:48.210
because that would have gone elsewhere. But
as you said this morning, NHS 111 started

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:53.569
as one thing and has morphed into something
else, and it’s probably unsustainable to

00:35:53.569 --> 00:36:00.660
keep that level of growth of that service.
It’s also – and I’m interested about

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:05.950
how we manage the channel and how we think
about that relative to digital, for instance,

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:11.240
and what – and how we industrialise what
we’ve built so far. If that’s not – that’s

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:13.430
a terrible word for a health setting, but
you know what I mean.

00:36:13.430 --> 00:36:16.119
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: No, I know what you mean,
and there’s probably about half a dozen

00:36:16.119 --> 00:36:19.940
really complicated questions inside that one.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Sorry.

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:25.360
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: But – so in terms of
the digital versus telephone handling, my

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:30.500
expectation is that we will continue to see
double-digit growth in the telephone calls

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.979
for the next year or two, and then it will
flatten out, and then it should start to drop

00:36:33.979 --> 00:36:40.309
away as the app and online increasingly replaces
it. We’ve not really done this transformation

00:36:40.309 --> 00:36:44.599
in the NHS before, so it’s hard to know
how swift the uptake will be and what the

00:36:44.599 --> 00:36:49.910
curve will look like as people get used to
a different way of accessing it.

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:56.630
But I think the bigger question is, we’ve
moved from 111 basically being a triage service

00:36:56.630 --> 00:37:01.720
where you ring up and they guide you to who
you should go and talk to, to 111 focusing

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:06.500
on trying to solve your problem for you. So,
sometimes, they solve your problem by telling

00:37:06.500 --> 00:37:10.780
you where else you need to go, but more and
more often, more than half the patients who

00:37:10.780 --> 00:37:16.269
ring us get to speak to a clinician or get
a clinical input into it, and we will – increasingly,

00:37:16.269 --> 00:37:18.920
you’ll be able to get your prescription
by ringing 111, or you’ll be able to get

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:25.029
an appointment by ringing 111. So, as we move
in that direction, we do need to change and

00:37:25.029 --> 00:37:30.190
are going to have to work through the contracts
that we let to be clear that growth in demand

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:34.950
is a good thing in this part of the health
system, and that therefore, the contracts

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:37.910
need to encourage high quality as a service,
rapid accessing, answering of the phone and

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:43.400
all of those sorts of things in this area,
and that’s going to require us to, as we

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:47.599
are reletting the contracts, be a bit smart
– be smart about what we’re asking people

00:37:47.599 --> 00:37:51.249
for, for the future.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And do we get access to the

00:37:51.249 --> 00:37:55.819
data as to what’s being presented, what
happens, where people go, etc.?

00:37:55.819 --> 00:37:58.089
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Because that’s gold dust

00:37:58.089 --> 00:38:00.859
in terms of then thinking about intervention
and prevention.

00:38:00.859 --> 00:38:05.420
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah, no, we have fantastic
data now. About 18 months ago, we commissioned

00:38:05.420 --> 00:38:10.509
through – in fact, through the North East
CSU that David went to visit, a project called

00:38:10.509 --> 00:38:19.779
Radar, which takes all of the data out of
the other 111s, tracks what the advice was

00:38:19.779 --> 00:38:24.119
that was given to the patient, and then tracks
where they actually went. So, we can see if

00:38:24.119 --> 00:38:27.609
someone was told to go – how many people
were told to go to a pharmacy, and how many

00:38:27.609 --> 00:38:31.970
of those people actually pitched up in the
A&amp;E anyway. So, increasingly, that’s part

00:38:31.970 --> 00:38:37.650
of us modelling whether the digital algorithms
that we’re using are picking up the right

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:40.019
patients and giving the right advice to the
right people.

00:38:40.019 --> 00:38:45.570
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay, great.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Matt, on finance.

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:51.789
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Yes, so I’m presenting
a month-ten finance update, probably the last

00:38:51.789 --> 00:38:58.170
time we’ll cover just the commissioning
sector in one of our Board meetings. Our month-ten

00:38:58.170 --> 00:39:02.849
forecast outturn for the commissioning sector
for the year as a whole was a surplus of £706

00:39:02.849 --> 00:39:09.319
million against a planned surplus of £265
million. Within that, we anticipate that by

00:39:09.319 --> 00:39:15.780
the end of the year, we’ll have an overspend
in the CCGs of around £250 million spread

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:22.579
across about 35 CCGs, driven largely by overperformance
on acute contracts and by under-delivery on

00:39:22.579 --> 00:39:27.839
QIPP. As has been the pattern in previous
years, we will see that offset by underspends

00:39:27.839 --> 00:39:33.820
in some of our direct commissioning budgets
and some of our central, nationally held budgets,

00:39:33.820 --> 00:39:37.900
too.
I think the key thing for the boards to be

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:43.890
aware of is that, on a risk-adjusted basis,
we are confident that the overall surplus

00:39:43.890 --> 00:39:47.660
on the commissioner side will be sufficient
to balance the NHS group as a whole for the

00:39:47.660 --> 00:39:55.461
year 2018/19, and we’re clearly – I’m
working very closely with Elizabeth on the

00:39:55.461 --> 00:40:03.249
NHSI side to ensure that we land the year
in that fashion. We’re also focusing on

00:40:03.249 --> 00:40:10.410
understanding the underlying causes of those
CCG deteriorations in year in financial performance,

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:16.210
to ensure that we can set sustainable plans
for – in those CCGs and in, indeed, all

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:20.740
commissioners and providers for the year ahead,
as the first year of the sort of long-term

00:40:20.740 --> 00:40:25.920
plan period. And given the decisions that
we’ve taken to rebase control totals onto

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:29.410
a stretching but achievable basis, and not
to hold very material reserves for ‘19/20,

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:36.369
that work to understand those underlying causes
and ensure that they’re addressed in ‘19/20

00:40:36.369 --> 00:40:38.719
plans is absolutely crucial in the coming
weeks.

00:40:38.719 --> 00:40:45.499
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Julian, any observations you’d
like to make as a – in – having been here

00:40:45.499 --> 00:40:49.910
for just over a week?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: No, probably on this, not

00:40:49.910 --> 00:40:54.950
really. I mean, as Matt said, there’s quite
a lot of work to do just to coordinate closely

00:40:54.950 --> 00:40:59.869
between he and I over the next kind of month
as we close the accounts.

00:40:59.869 --> 00:41:02.910
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any questions for Matt? No?
Excellent.

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:08.640
So, item five, Emily. Where’s – I can’t
– where is Emily? Oh, Emily, there you are.

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:18.569
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Not quite as far away as Pauline
was. So, the paper in item five presents the

00:41:18.569 --> 00:41:25.220
NHS England and NHS Improvement funding and
resource ‘19/20 plan: supporting the NHS

00:41:25.220 --> 00:41:32.430
long-term plan. And the document is provided
in an annexe, and there’s a covering paper.

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:38.420
Really, we’re just asking the Board to do
three things. One is to recommend the publication

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:44.500
of this document on the 1st of April on the
NHS England website. Secondly, to note the

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:51.049
’19/20 allocations and to ratify the £305
million allocation of our capital mandate.

00:41:51.049 --> 00:41:57.180
And third, to delegate any in-year adjustments
required to the CEO and CFO, recognising that

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:02.650
in bringing the two organisations together,
we’ve had to make some judgements about

00:42:02.650 --> 00:42:06.549
how to create combined directorate budgets,
and we expect that as the operating model

00:42:06.549 --> 00:42:10.339
– the detailed design is done during the
year, we may actually need to make some adjustments

00:42:10.339 --> 00:42:16.490
and move funding between directorates in order
to make sure we can meet our goals.

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:21.719
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Maybe this is a question for
Simon rather than you, Emily, but I think

00:42:21.719 --> 00:42:27.569
it’s now for the second year running, life
expectancy has stalled, and the figures were

00:42:27.569 --> 00:42:31.440
out yesterday, and you know, health inequalities
are such an important part of our plan, going

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:37.799
forward. Is – I mean, is this a trend or
is this – I mean, it’s two years now.

00:42:37.799 --> 00:42:43.670
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think we can now conclude
it is a trend. The ONS data that you’re

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:47.690
talking about, David, particularly, found
a statistically significant reduction in life

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:52.609
expectancy for women in poorer areas of England,
by contrast with women in better-off areas

00:42:52.609 --> 00:42:58.540
in England. The numbers in some other parts
of the UK were not sizeable enough to be able

00:42:58.540 --> 00:43:03.930
to draw statistical significance from. But
yes, I think that clearly is the case, and

00:43:03.930 --> 00:43:09.019
we’ve had the discussion previously with
the evidence review from PHE, and as part

00:43:09.019 --> 00:43:14.739
of our LTP implementation, we are requiring
each part of the country to show how they

00:43:14.739 --> 00:43:20.680
are using the extra inequalities-related funding
they’re getting to deliver some of the best-buy

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:27.579
inequalities reductions that were in the long-term
plan itself. But all of – with each sort

00:43:27.579 --> 00:43:34.009
of successive publication, there were some
material that the actuaries put out a few

00:43:34.009 --> 00:43:38.730
weeks ago looking life expectancy at 65 rather
than life expectancy at birth, which is what

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:44.039
the ONS data are, is all kind of confirmatory
that that is what is happening. I think – from

00:43:44.039 --> 00:43:52.059
memory, I think the ONS data are for the period
2012 to 2015 to ’17, so there’s a lag

00:43:52.059 --> 00:43:55.460
on these things, obviously, but that does
definitely appear to be the case.

00:43:55.460 --> 00:44:00.930
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I’ve been struck – I mean,
Matthew may want to comment. I mean, talking

00:44:00.930 --> 00:44:06.479
to integrated care systems, there is a growing
focus on the social determinants of care and

00:44:06.479 --> 00:44:11.859
on inequalities, which I don’t think was
there to such a great extent even two or three

00:44:11.859 --> 00:44:16.559
years ago. Is that right, or…?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: No, I think that’s very

00:44:16.559 --> 00:44:23.130
fair. The – all of the STPs have had at
their core – and I think it’s partly the

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:27.559
upside of having local authorities involved
in these discussions, as well – the sense

00:44:27.559 --> 00:44:32.660
of what is the population that you’re serving
and what are the health needs, and inequalities

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:37.869
is playing out in that. We’ve also, since
we started doing a lot more work on looking

00:44:37.869 --> 00:44:42.819
at the inequalities impact of things that
we’re doing and developing ways of measuring

00:44:42.819 --> 00:44:48.430
whether what we’re doing is widening or
closing the impact. So, I think just generally,

00:44:48.430 --> 00:44:53.829
the NHS is much more focused on this, although
I think there’s still a lot to learn about

00:44:53.829 --> 00:44:57.779
what is the right thing to do in order to
deliver the output you want.

00:44:57.779 --> 00:45:07.150
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Any questions for Emily?
Can we approve the paper then, please? Oh,

00:45:07.150 --> 00:45:10.980
sorry.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: As Emily said, this is our

00:45:10.980 --> 00:45:16.019
sort of going in position for the 1st of April.
We will obviously be doing significant further

00:45:16.019 --> 00:45:21.859
work over the next four to eight weeks as
part of the bringing together of E and I,

00:45:21.859 --> 00:45:26.109
the leadership transitions that are in train
in various parts of the organisation. So,

00:45:26.109 --> 00:45:30.440
we’ll coming back to you, as we discussed
this morning, during May with a refresh on

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:35.630
what this looks like, but we have a legal
obligation to publish this material before

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:39.269
the end of the financial year, and this is
what we’re doing this afternoon.

00:45:39.269 --> 00:45:44.150
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yeah. So, we’ll have a detailed
discussion as the next Delivery, Quality and

00:45:44.150 --> 00:45:47.539
Performance Committee, and then at the next
joint board, we’ll bring it back, okay.

00:45:47.539 --> 00:45:50.519
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Exactly, yeah.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Thank you. For our next

00:45:50.519 --> 00:45:53.410
item, over to you, Dido.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Well, I suspect I’ll just

00:45:53.410 --> 00:46:01.099
pass straight over to Julian. Julian’s been
working with me on the workforce implementation

00:46:01.099 --> 00:46:06.299
plan, which we both prefer to call the NHS
people plan. And I think, Julian, you’re

00:46:06.299 --> 00:46:13.240
going to give us a verbal update of progress.
We are due to publish an interim plan during

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:17.559
April, so over to you, Julian.
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: Thank you very much, thank

00:46:17.559 --> 00:46:24.650
you. I’ll start with a reminder that on
the publication of the long-term plan it was

00:46:24.650 --> 00:46:28.300
announced we would have a workforce implementation
plan that would support the delivery of the

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:32.999
long-term plan, with interim recommendations
in April, and then the full report to follow

00:46:32.999 --> 00:46:39.759
in the autumn. And as Dido said, I’ve been
working alongside Dido with a number of colleagues

00:46:39.759 --> 00:46:46.130
to – over the last nine weeks to pull together
the interim report. I think it’s important

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:53.690
to say, first of all, about the approach we’ve
taken with this. We’ve established, rightly

00:46:53.690 --> 00:46:58.829
I think, a very inclusive, wide-ranging group
of colleagues that have come together in service

00:46:58.829 --> 00:47:04.490
of ensuring we do justice to the range of
activities going on in relation to workforce

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:09.880
and staffing across the NHS. And that starts
with the steering group, which is a collection

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:17.010
of around 50 colleagues from across the arm’s-length
bodies, royal colleges, staff-side organisations,

00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:22.930
NHS colleagues, special interest groups; everyone
who has an interest in workforce across the

00:47:22.930 --> 00:47:26.410
pantheon of health and care is involved in
this work.

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:31.900
That in turn is supported by a working group,
a smaller group of around 20 colleagues that’s

00:47:31.900 --> 00:47:39.700
met weekly since we started in mid- to late
January. And then we have the engine room,

00:47:39.700 --> 00:47:47.289
if you like, of the five key groups. First
one being the future medical workforce chaired

00:47:47.289 --> 00:47:54.930
by Steve Powis, and also supported by Sara
Hurley on dental. The future clinical workforce

00:47:54.930 --> 00:48:00.890
chaired by Ruth May, also including Suzanne
Rastrick on AHPs, Keith Ridge on pharmacy,

00:48:00.890 --> 00:48:07.579
Sue Hill on healthcare scientists. Making
the NHS the best place to work, which Dido

00:48:07.579 --> 00:48:13.869
referenced in her introduction, given feedback
from colleagues as you’ve been out and about:

00:48:13.869 --> 00:48:18.329
key part of the work. That’s chaired by
Navina Evans, Chief Executive of East London

00:48:18.329 --> 00:48:26.920
Foundation Trust. Leadership talent development
chaired by Peter Homer, and technical skills,

00:48:26.920 --> 00:48:32.829
digital enablement chaired by Sir David Behan,
Chair of Health Education England. Those five

00:48:32.829 --> 00:48:38.609
groups supported by cross-cutting and enabling
workstreams on workforce analysis, workforce

00:48:38.609 --> 00:48:43.670
architecture, what part of the system does
what in relation to workforce planning and

00:48:43.670 --> 00:48:47.150
strategy, transformation and roles, and international
recruitment.

00:48:47.150 --> 00:48:50.940
Now, that’s the architecture of the – if
you like, the framework and the project plan,

00:48:50.940 --> 00:48:57.759
but doesn’t quite do justice to the sense
of how we’ve gone about this work. And I’m

00:48:57.759 --> 00:49:03.289
struck by your opening remarks, Chairman,
about your visits and about how – very much

00:49:03.289 --> 00:49:08.849
about relationships, where things work well
in terms of integrated care systems, and also,

00:49:08.849 --> 00:49:16.559
Pauline and Simon have paid tribute to NHS
staff in pressing times delivering extraordinary

00:49:16.559 --> 00:49:21.910
results. And if ever there was a reminder,
it is all about the people, and that’s the

00:49:21.910 --> 00:49:27.550
spirit in which we’ve pursued the people
plan. And at the heart of the people plan

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:33.180
is the hypothesis which is based on significant
research and evidence, and almost exactly

00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:39.180
a year ago, the report that was commissioned
by NHS England into the relationship between

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:44.620
staff engagement and productivity, led by
Michael West and the King’s Fund, which

00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:49.450
concluded that high levels of staff engagement
are associated with low rates of staff sickness

00:49:49.450 --> 00:49:54.039
and absence, and therefore lower rates of
bank and agency staff. The size of that effect

00:49:54.039 --> 00:50:00.890
is significant. An average upward shift of
only 0.1% on scores on a five-point scale

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:05.800
of staff engagement is associated with a saving,
an average saving on agency bank spend of

00:50:05.800 --> 00:50:10.940
£1.7 million.
Now, I think that’s just the start, really,

00:50:10.940 --> 00:50:19.200
of the opportunity and the scale of what we
can achieve if we pay real attention to our

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:24.789
people and the engagement of our staff, and
just to reinforce that point, if we draw the

00:50:24.789 --> 00:50:32.140
lessons from the CQC NHSI use of resources
assessments, it’s clear that those trusts

00:50:32.140 --> 00:50:39.809
that are rated outstanding, all of them reside
in the top quartile for staff engagement.

00:50:39.809 --> 00:50:45.499
All of them, top-quartile performance on pay
costs per weighted activity unit. Top quartile

00:50:45.499 --> 00:50:53.729
on reduction in agency spend, retention rates,
lowest sickness absence. Significant advances

00:50:53.729 --> 00:50:58.809
and leading the charge on use of technology
and digital innovation. All have in common

00:50:58.809 --> 00:51:04.069
a clear and systematic improvement method,
positive culture and leadership, and delivering

00:51:04.069 --> 00:51:07.680
financial sustainability. And given the challenges
that many of our organisations are facing

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:14.180
at present, these are the ingredients, really,
that we need if we’re to move the dial on

00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:18.960
a range of performance issues. But the starting
point for our plan is about the importance

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:26.259
of the engagement of our people. Because the
importance of that cannot be overstated in

00:51:26.259 --> 00:51:31.480
relation to the delivery of high-quality and
sustainable care.

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:39.140
So, we’ve taken an approach where we’ve
attempted to engage far and wide across both

00:51:39.140 --> 00:51:42.859
obviously arm’s-length bodies and nationally,
but also across the service. We’ve used

00:51:42.859 --> 00:51:48.719
a range of channels, including weekly e-bulletins,
that all the chairs have contributed to and

00:51:48.719 --> 00:51:56.250
been part of. We’ve had an interactive space
on the Department of Health and Social Care’s

00:51:56.250 --> 00:52:03.730
site, Talk Health and Care, where we’ve
had significant ideas and discussions and

00:52:03.730 --> 00:52:09.609
comments and feedback. We’ve used the steering
groups, stakeholder events. We’ve had several

00:52:09.609 --> 00:52:17.290
roundtables with colleagues across the whole
NHS in different sectors and different roles,

00:52:17.290 --> 00:52:25.259
nursing directors, acute and specialist CEOs,
acute CEOs, community, HRDs and so on. And

00:52:25.259 --> 00:52:32.059
I think most encouragingly, we’ve had a
very strong response to the letter that Dido

00:52:32.059 --> 00:52:38.469
and I sent out on the 6th of March, which
listed out some of the key themes that were

00:52:38.469 --> 00:52:48.459
emerging. We’ve had 105 responses representing
over 250 organisations, all of whom have shared

00:52:48.459 --> 00:52:56.559
both their views and examples of good practice,
and all their – really, their reinforcing

00:52:56.559 --> 00:53:01.969
of the point about the importance of engagement
of our people. And in terms of the themes

00:53:01.969 --> 00:53:08.789
that have come back to us based on an analysis
of those responses, making the NHS a better

00:53:08.789 --> 00:53:16.839
place to work comes out as a clear theme and
will be a big part of the work that we do

00:53:16.839 --> 00:53:24.219
and see in the report. The emphasis on flexible
working, more support on health and wellbeing.

00:53:24.219 --> 00:53:30.559
Only a few weeks ago, we saw the publication
of the Pearson Report into the mental wellbeing

00:53:30.559 --> 00:53:34.509
of learners and staff within the NHS, which
had some important things to say about what

00:53:34.509 --> 00:53:39.839
we can do more work on. The need to look at
different generational needs, particularly

00:53:39.839 --> 00:53:45.010
in relation to the development of flexibility
and technology. The importance of portfolio

00:53:45.010 --> 00:53:50.650
careers, and the crucial underpinning importance
of engaging our people.

00:53:50.650 --> 00:53:57.520
Secondly, the importance of the right leadership
culture at all levels of the service, particularly

00:53:57.520 --> 00:54:03.450
line managers, who significantly influence
the experience of our staff across the service.

00:54:03.450 --> 00:54:09.920
The importance of central bodies exhibiting
the leadership they want to see. That was

00:54:09.920 --> 00:54:19.150
stressed very much as part of the response
we saw. And out of the process of engagement,

00:54:19.150 --> 00:54:25.219
one theme came out as urgent that we require
significant action on over the next few months,

00:54:25.219 --> 00:54:30.369
and that is the challenges in nursing. And
that’s something where we’ve spent a lot

00:54:30.369 --> 00:54:38.619
of time with Ruth and her team, and colleagues
in HEE and DHSE and NHSE and NHSI, really

00:54:38.619 --> 00:54:43.700
looking hard at what can we do to move the
dial on this over the next few months, as

00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:48.930
well as thinking about the sustainable solutions
moving forward. And that’s something which

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:55.819
we are working through the specific interventions
that are going to help us make progress in

00:54:55.819 --> 00:55:01.069
that area. Yeah?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Just from a nursing perspective,

00:55:01.069 --> 00:55:04.611
Ruth. Give us your –
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Absolutely. So, I had the privilege

00:55:04.611 --> 00:55:11.900
to hold my very first CNO summit middle of
March, and I’m thankful for members of – around

00:55:11.900 --> 00:55:17.060
this table that were there, particularly Julian
and Dido. And the discussion, the whole of

00:55:17.060 --> 00:55:21.329
the second day was about workforce, because
it is the single biggest challenge that a

00:55:21.329 --> 00:55:25.150
director of nursing – and whether it’s
a ward sister, the community team leaders

00:55:25.150 --> 00:55:31.650
facing it any day, 24 hours a day, it is,
how many nurses have I got, what is the nurse

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:37.640
makeup, their skill, their experience, their
expertise, their background. So, it is by

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:43.369
far the biggest challenge that nurses are
facing, nurse leaders are facing, and, indeed,

00:55:43.369 --> 00:55:48.839
me now as a new Chief Nursing Officer. So,
we welcome the discussion. We welcome the

00:55:48.839 --> 00:55:54.160
honesty and the frankness by which we were
able to have that discussion about some of

00:55:54.160 --> 00:56:00.869
those challenges. And what we were working
with directors of nursing around was the details

00:56:00.869 --> 00:56:06.529
of what potentially some of those solutions
could be as part of Dido’s and Julian’s

00:56:06.529 --> 00:56:12.239
report, really – it really resonated with
the nurse leaders when we were in Birmingham

00:56:12.239 --> 00:56:17.569
a couple of weeks ago.
So, lots of work being done. Lots of this

00:56:17.569 --> 00:56:23.009
work has been done previously. Now is an opportunity
to really – for me to land some of this,

00:56:23.009 --> 00:56:28.430
so that we can make a real difference. And
not just in the long term, but what we can

00:56:28.430 --> 00:56:34.390
do now, this month, next month, right up to
September, about how we get more potential

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:39.020
nurses coming through on the graduate scheme
starting in this September. So, we want to

00:56:39.020 --> 00:56:43.930
do things that actually land right now, as
well as into the future.

00:56:43.930 --> 00:56:50.249
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ian, did you want to give any
reply?

00:56:50.249 --> 00:56:58.609
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: No, I think the point that had
been made about a participative, engaging

00:56:58.609 --> 00:57:04.160
approach, I think I’d want to echo. I think
Julian said it with distinction, if I may

00:57:04.160 --> 00:57:08.779
say, and I pay tribute to him during this
period of time. I think there’s two issues.

00:57:08.779 --> 00:57:13.700
I think there’s one that Ruth has just made
about, what is it that we need to do now?

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:17.719
I think the covering report flags up some
important issues on nursing around shortages

00:57:17.719 --> 00:57:22.339
in learning disabilities, just to connect
back to the report that Matthew took us through.

00:57:22.339 --> 00:57:28.190
Mental health; I think there’s geographical
variation in this. So, how we begin to connect

00:57:28.190 --> 00:57:33.049
with the universities, further education institutions,
to actually begin to do something about this

00:57:33.049 --> 00:57:40.219
I think is critical. I personally think we
need to sort out CPD. 50% of our current staff

00:57:40.219 --> 00:57:47.229
will be working for the NHS in 20 years’
time. I think we use CPDs sloppily and interchangeably;

00:57:47.229 --> 00:57:53.009
we confuse upskilling, which HEE has funded,
with what the employer’s responsibilities

00:57:53.009 --> 00:57:57.800
are, what people need to do to continue to
revalidate and use their licence. So, I hope

00:57:57.800 --> 00:58:03.029
as we can go through this next period, that
we can make progress on this.

00:58:03.029 --> 00:58:08.329
I think from my point of view, I’ve been
around for a while, and I think there’s

00:58:08.329 --> 00:58:12.319
conversations taking place on workforce that
I’ve not seen take place before. I think

00:58:12.319 --> 00:58:18.640
people are working together, and I think it’s
provided a platform of – that we’re able

00:58:18.640 --> 00:58:22.950
to build consensus and some agreement about
what the next steps are, whether that’s

00:58:22.950 --> 00:58:28.270
the work that Steve’s been done on medical
education, and Ruth on nursing, some of the

00:58:28.270 --> 00:58:34.061
challenges around technology and what that
means not just for clinical staff, but for

00:58:34.061 --> 00:58:38.559
how many analysts we’ll need in the next
15 to 20 years as well. So, I think Julian’s

00:58:38.559 --> 00:58:44.079
done a fantastic job, and the interim report
will provide a platform on which we can build

00:58:44.079 --> 00:58:48.640
beyond ‘19/20.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Maybe it’s just worth sort

00:58:48.640 --> 00:58:57.450
of just to be clear, the interim report will
aim to set out a more detailed vision, building

00:58:57.450 --> 00:59:02.190
on what was in the long-term plan, so a more
detailed vision for our people for the next

00:59:02.190 --> 00:59:08.709
ten years. It will also aim to set out some
practical actions that we intend to take now.

00:59:08.709 --> 00:59:13.140
In fact, some of the teams are just getting
on with doing it now. But be really clear

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:17.999
about what we’re going to do in the next
calendar year. And then thirdly, it will set

00:59:17.999 --> 00:59:24.049
out what additional collaborative work we
need to do to build the full people plan.

00:59:24.049 --> 00:59:28.329
And I keep saying to the teams: this is not
a crash diet. This is a new way of life. We

00:59:28.329 --> 00:59:33.499
will need to keep working together on this
across the different arm’s-length bodies,

00:59:33.499 --> 00:59:37.910
with all the royal colleges, the unions, all
of our people. Because actually, thinking

00:59:37.910 --> 00:59:44.359
about how we evolve our workforce is probably
the most important job of leading the NHS.

00:59:44.359 --> 00:59:49.079
So, it’s so exciting to see everyone actually
working together on it, but, you know, we

00:59:49.079 --> 00:59:52.200
will only have the beginnings of that when
we publish the interim report; there’s an

00:59:52.200 --> 00:59:58.269
awful lot more work to keep doing.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think the work that Julian’s

00:59:58.269 --> 01:00:05.410
done has been fantastic, and we’re doing
a lot of work at a really high level in the

01:00:05.410 --> 01:00:12.789
NHS. For me, the real acid test is going to
be: does a typical NHS board regard workforce

01:00:12.789 --> 01:00:14.959
in the same status as finance?
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Absolutely.

01:00:14.959 --> 01:00:20.249
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, we would expect to see
an audit committee as part of a board structure,

01:00:20.249 --> 01:00:24.349
but I bet you very few have got a workforce
committee that’s looking at the workforce

01:00:24.349 --> 01:00:29.640
needs of their particular organisation going
forward. So, anything we can do to promote

01:00:29.640 --> 01:00:33.980
that I think is really important, because
this has got to be bottom-up, and if we can’t

01:00:33.980 --> 01:00:40.089
get the trusts engaged in this, we’ll lose
it. We’ll create this great, fantastic architecture

01:00:40.089 --> 01:00:44.880
up here, but it won’t connect to what people
perceive on the ground as being the real need.

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:48.869
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Well said.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Noel?

01:00:48.869 --> 01:00:56.069
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I thought your theme four,
Julian, in your deck, had a number of really

01:00:56.069 --> 01:01:05.420
important ideas in it. One of them is around
how we equip clinical staff to take on managerial

01:01:05.420 --> 01:01:10.469
roles, and in particular leadership roles.
And if you look back, you know, we’ve relied

01:01:10.469 --> 01:01:18.589
hugely on the NHS Leadership Academy to help
prepare clinicians for the huge amount of

01:01:18.589 --> 01:01:21.049
change: ICSs, digital, the whole lot.
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: Yeah.

01:01:21.049 --> 01:01:24.640
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: How much emphasis do you think
that will get in your final recommendations?

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:32.510
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: I think it’s central,
Noel. I think that the way in which we – I

01:01:32.510 --> 01:01:42.289
think we’ve seen positive shifts across
the NHS over the last ten years or so in clinicians

01:01:42.289 --> 01:01:47.670
taking on leadership roles. I think we’re
seeing more – certainly in my own organisation,

01:01:47.670 --> 01:01:54.739
the new generation of clinical directors gives
me a lot of optimism about the way that younger

01:01:54.739 --> 01:02:00.449
clinicians are really motivated by getting
involved in improvement science. And many

01:02:00.449 --> 01:02:05.799
of our junior doctors get involved in the
work we do on the Leeds Improvement Method,

01:02:05.799 --> 01:02:09.979
and I see this in other trusts as well. And
Kathy, the work that we’re doing as part

01:02:09.979 --> 01:02:14.759
of the VM[?] cohort. But indeed, the other
trusts that are involved in the vital signs

01:02:14.759 --> 01:02:19.229
work, we’re seeing the engagement and involvement
of many clinicians in that work, because the

01:02:19.229 --> 01:02:27.369
systematic, evidence-based approach to this
resonates I think with many of our clinicians

01:02:27.369 --> 01:02:32.599
across many professions. And I think we need
to encourage that; I’m sure we’ll be saying

01:02:32.599 --> 01:02:36.809
that.
Leadership in the round, though, is important

01:02:36.809 --> 01:02:43.759
across all backgrounds, and I think the way
in which we’re able to put a premium on

01:02:43.759 --> 01:02:49.859
values and behaviours as part of this is important,
particularly the exhortation that we have

01:02:49.859 --> 01:02:59.349
from colleagues in Virginia Mason, which is
all about how we add value to our patients’

01:02:59.349 --> 01:03:09.319
experience and how we respect one another
and how leaders – and I can picture the

01:03:09.319 --> 01:03:16.039
Taiichi Ohno sketch: big eyes, big ears, small
mouth. And I think these are the things that

01:03:16.039 --> 01:03:22.390
kind of resonate in terms of learning some
of these methods that do actually, genuinely

01:03:22.390 --> 01:03:28.039
make a big difference to the way that front-line
teams are able to feel empowered, get involved

01:03:28.039 --> 01:03:35.099
in changing patient pathways, removing waste
and adding value to the experience of our

01:03:35.099 --> 01:03:37.519
patients. And clinical leadership is fundamental
to that.

01:03:37.519 --> 01:03:39.849
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Very good. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Kathy.

01:03:39.849 --> 01:03:44.220
&gt;&gt;Kathy McLean: Just one thing, just sort
of building on that and what Andrew said about

01:03:44.220 --> 01:03:49.759
making sure it’s at the front line; I would
just add to that that sometimes those things

01:03:49.759 --> 01:03:57.880
are actually done most by those who are most
able, bigger and probably have the least issues.

01:03:57.880 --> 01:04:03.410
And I just wonder as you go forward, being
proactive at the front line is supporting

01:04:03.410 --> 01:04:08.699
the most challenged in terms of geography,
in terms of ability to get workforce, to actually

01:04:08.699 --> 01:04:14.989
embrace this with a bit of help earlier than
the others, so you actually change that dynamic,

01:04:14.989 --> 01:04:16.589
which otherwise there’s always a lag.
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: Yeah.

01:04:16.589 --> 01:04:20.199
&gt;&gt;Kathy McLean: And there’s a lag in the
place that needs it most, very often.

01:04:20.199 --> 01:04:23.920
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good work, Julian. Thank you
very much for coming today. You’ve obviously

01:04:23.920 --> 01:04:24.920
done a great job.
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: Thank you.

01:04:24.920 --> 01:04:26.150
&gt;&gt;David Prior: All the feedback has been incredibly
positive.

01:04:26.150 --> 01:04:30.099
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So thank you very much.

01:04:30.099 --> 01:04:33.699
&gt;&gt;Julian Hartley: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Establishing integrated

01:04:33.699 --> 01:04:37.759
care systems. Matthew?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you. Yes, let’s

01:04:37.759 --> 01:04:41.309
– so, let me give you an update on where
we’ve got to on integrated care systems.

01:04:41.309 --> 01:04:47.880
If we see in the long-term plan, we committed
that by April 2021, integrated care systems

01:04:47.880 --> 01:04:55.130
would cover the whole of the country. It’s
probably just worth asking why – why have

01:04:55.130 --> 01:05:02.339
we put this at the core of the long-term plan
– and think about what we consider to be

01:05:02.339 --> 01:05:08.460
the big challenges facing the NHS going forward,
particularly around prevention, around an

01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:12.739
ageing population, around the need to support
people with long-term conditions, and frequently

01:05:12.739 --> 01:05:19.509
multiple long-term conditions, and increasing
the ability of people to self-care. And that

01:05:19.509 --> 01:05:25.939
requires us to take a systemic view and a
system-wide view in partnership with other

01:05:25.939 --> 01:05:30.339
organisations, in partnership with local government
when we’re thinking about the things that

01:05:30.339 --> 01:05:35.670
drive ill health, when we think about housing
or we think about education, and in partnership

01:05:35.670 --> 01:05:43.319
with the voluntary sector which is delivering
support to many of these people at large scale

01:05:43.319 --> 01:05:49.449
across the country.
So, we’ve created the integrated care systems

01:05:49.449 --> 01:05:58.719
to be integrated and to integrate the care
around individuals. And we want them to be

01:05:58.719 --> 01:06:05.420
able to look across their populations and
take responsibility for managing the resources,

01:06:05.420 --> 01:06:10.890
improving quality and access and outcome,
and focusing on the reduction in inequality,

01:06:10.890 --> 01:06:17.599
by building partnerships with other organisations
and by driving the complex system changes

01:06:17.599 --> 01:06:24.269
that we’re going to need that will change
us from the acute hospital being the centre

01:06:24.269 --> 01:06:30.119
of a web by which things get sucked into,
to saying: no, acute hospitals are there for

01:06:30.119 --> 01:06:34.410
a specific thing, for a specific group of
patients at a specific moment in their lives,

01:06:34.410 --> 01:06:38.619
and the ambition of the NHS should be to keep
people out of hospital and keep them healthy

01:06:38.619 --> 01:06:42.849
or keep them managing their own condition
and maintain their liberty.

01:06:42.849 --> 01:06:48.989
And in order to do that, we need to have a
structure which starts not at the million

01:06:48.989 --> 01:06:55.150
people in a system, but starts at the 30,000
to 50,000 people on a housing estate who are

01:06:55.150 --> 01:07:00.079
supported by a community mental health team,
a community social care team and a primary

01:07:00.079 --> 01:07:08.650
care network, and how do we engage in the
particular needs of a community? And when

01:07:08.650 --> 01:07:13.759
that works well, we’ll find that there are
networks of networks that build up, probably

01:07:13.759 --> 01:07:20.299
at the level of town, what local authorities
call ‘place’, but at the level of: what

01:07:20.299 --> 01:07:26.789
do the people of Bradford or Barnsley or Brighton
or Bournemouth need in order to maintain their

01:07:26.789 --> 01:07:30.969
health, and what decisions can you not take
at the size of half a million people, but

01:07:30.969 --> 01:07:34.920
you actually need to think at the size of
a million people because you need to decide

01:07:34.920 --> 01:07:40.460
how many tertiary trauma centres you need
or you need to decide what your stroke unit’s

01:07:40.460 --> 01:07:43.980
going to be or you need to start to decide
a workforce plan at a population that’s

01:07:43.980 --> 01:07:51.200
large enough to make a difference.
So, we are working to put in place across

01:07:51.200 --> 01:07:56.579
the country that architecture that starts
not at the top, but that starts at the bottom.

01:07:56.579 --> 01:08:01.890
It starts at the neighbourhood, builds into
a place and engages with partners and then

01:08:01.890 --> 01:08:07.170
thinks at a system level when it needs to.
And our best integrated care systems say that

01:08:07.170 --> 01:08:11.760
80% of the decisions are taken locally and
that 20% makes its way up to the top, not

01:08:11.760 --> 01:08:17.150
the other way around. And I think that is
a philosophical approach.

01:08:17.150 --> 01:08:25.630
We are challenging them as part of the long-term
plan design to rethink out-of-hospital care

01:08:25.630 --> 01:08:32.520
to bring together the pieces, so that an individual
is treated around their needs rather than

01:08:32.520 --> 01:08:36.790
multiple different services arriving sometimes
one in the morning, one at lunchtime, one

01:08:36.790 --> 01:08:40.409
in the evening doing bits of services, and
then you don’t see anybody for three days

01:08:40.409 --> 01:08:46.330
and then someone else turns up. How do we
integrate care around people’s homes and

01:08:46.330 --> 01:08:51.639
people’s lives? How do we reduce the pressure
on acute services? How do we move away from

01:08:51.639 --> 01:08:58.619
having to open more beds every year because
we can manage people closer to their homes?

01:08:58.619 --> 01:09:03.449
How do we give people control over the decisions
about their own health, and give them their

01:09:03.449 --> 01:09:08.790
liberty back? The conversation that we had
about people with learning disabilities could

01:09:08.790 --> 01:09:14.809
just as easily be about the 30% of our hospital
beds who’ve got people in them who are frail

01:09:14.809 --> 01:09:19.069
and elderly, and are in hospital largely as
a place of safety, rather than because we’re

01:09:19.069 --> 01:09:23.540
doing anything to make them better. And a
better place of safety would be their own

01:09:23.540 --> 01:09:31.869
home, with the NHS wrapped around them there.
And we’re challenging ICSs to say, ‘So,

01:09:31.869 --> 01:09:35.199
what does that mean for population health
and what are the big transformations we need,

01:09:35.199 --> 01:09:41.730
both in the way we work and the way we use
technology in order to do that?’ We’ve

01:09:41.730 --> 01:09:54.530
now got 14 integrated care systems up and
running, of which eight of them are operating

01:09:54.530 --> 01:09:59.700
effectively in a devolved manner and another
six are in shadow format, and we use those

01:09:59.700 --> 01:10:05.760
14 to work with them to develop what they
can do, because this is a new model that we’re

01:10:05.760 --> 01:10:11.830
bringing in, but also to develop the learning
that we can spread to the 28 that are still

01:10:11.830 --> 01:10:16.881
to follow. So, none of them are the complete
entity that can deliver the vision that I’ve

01:10:16.881 --> 01:10:21.920
just given you. Many of them are on that journey,
but they also need to be able to work out

01:10:21.920 --> 01:10:26.280
how do we do it more simply behind us, how
do we create the right governance, the right

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:32.320
attitudes, the right behaviours, the right
financial flows in order to be able to achieve

01:10:32.320 --> 01:10:35.340
those?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: [inaudible] given us a good

01:10:35.340 --> 01:10:39.750
view of that. Can I just ask, Tim; you’ve
seen this in another part of the world. I

01:10:39.750 --> 01:10:43.239
mean, what’s your observations on how it’s
developing over here?

01:10:43.239 --> 01:10:51.489
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: It’s great. Thank you for
asking. Well, first of all, I’m a bit jealous,

01:10:51.489 --> 01:10:59.960
because what you’re planning at a national
scale is tremendous. And I see the ingredients,

01:10:59.960 --> 01:11:07.300
I see the work you’ve done in creating population-based
metrics, which is a critical component of

01:11:07.300 --> 01:11:15.659
this, and one could imagine in a future way,
population-based metrics being the, for lack

01:11:15.659 --> 01:11:24.389
of a better term, the methodology for accountability
for integrated care systems.

01:11:24.389 --> 01:11:32.219
It’s tough work. I’ll just underline one
thing that Matthew said, which is really easy

01:11:32.219 --> 01:11:37.530
to say and really difficult to do, which is
that you talked about the different services

01:11:37.530 --> 01:11:42.989
coming into the individual’s house. Each
one of those delivers a service which in and

01:11:42.989 --> 01:11:49.510
of itself is highly specialised, and therefore
needs to be a distinct service because it

01:11:49.510 --> 01:11:57.250
is highly specialised. Coordinating across
those is really, really challenging and it

01:11:57.250 --> 01:12:04.239
has to occur at every level of the system,
the mindset that you are not just about delivering

01:12:04.239 --> 01:12:11.489
your narrow service, which is maybe meals
to an elderly person that can’t get a meal

01:12:11.489 --> 01:12:16.340
by themselves otherwise, and coordinating
that with physical therapy, which is really

01:12:16.340 --> 01:12:24.670
an entirely different service and needs to
be distinct, and yet in the care of an individual

01:12:24.670 --> 01:12:34.480
needs to be coordinated. So, I see both: I’m
jealous of the vision and the plan; I see

01:12:34.480 --> 01:12:41.410
a lot of challenges in the implementation,
knowing of course that you’ve got the data

01:12:41.410 --> 01:12:47.090
or you’re beginning to put together the
data that will be necessary to judge whether

01:12:47.090 --> 01:12:50.940
or not you’re actually achieving those milestones.
So, thank you.

01:12:50.940 --> 01:12:55.500
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks. Anyone else? Any other
questions for…? Oh, Laura, sorry.

01:12:55.500 --> 01:13:02.070
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: I just wanted to ask Matthew
whether the work that’s trying to produce

01:13:02.070 --> 01:13:08.389
the LHCREs, so the shared data – are we
beginning to see that making a difference

01:13:08.389 --> 01:13:14.699
to those ICSs, or are they at the moment using
things that aren’t quite what we envisage

01:13:14.699 --> 01:13:19.480
LHCREs becoming? So I suppose, what’s the
meshing of the two and are we seeing it actually

01:13:19.480 --> 01:13:23.619
make a practical difference to the ability
to run integrated care?

01:13:23.619 --> 01:13:30.600
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: So, the latter. At the
moment, they’re sharing data in all sorts

01:13:30.600 --> 01:13:38.389
of ways in the Local Health and Care Records.
The first contract to deploy a Local Health

01:13:38.389 --> 01:13:44.870
and Care Record was let by the Thames Valley
area last week or the week before, so we’re

01:13:44.870 --> 01:13:49.840
at the early stages there. But what we are
seeing across integrated care systems and

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:54.389
across STPs is a more openness about sharing
the data that they’ve got in order to try

01:13:54.389 --> 01:13:58.429
and make population health planning possible.
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: Okay. Thanks.

01:13:58.429 --> 01:14:03.420
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any other…? Matthew, thanks
very much. I think you’re doing some great

01:14:03.420 --> 01:14:11.000
work in this area, and the data that we saw
a glimpse of this morning is just fantastic,

01:14:11.000 --> 01:14:18.290
I think. So, the NHS Assembly. Neil – where’s
Neil? Oh, Neil, you’ve moved out there.

01:14:18.290 --> 01:14:24.810
Good. Neil, over to you.
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Okay. So, thank you. So,

01:14:24.810 --> 01:14:30.369
when we talked to stakeholders about the NHS
Assembly, there was strong consensus that

01:14:30.369 --> 01:14:36.000
it should be round about 50 people, but no
one envied us the task of trying to work out

01:14:36.000 --> 01:14:42.840
which 50 people. And so, it’s been quite
an incredible process. And we’ve had some

01:14:42.840 --> 01:14:48.530
fantastic applications, so over 500 people
from different walks of life involved in the

01:14:48.530 --> 01:14:53.940
NHS. And if you remember, the Board had a
vision of making this a really diverse and

01:14:53.940 --> 01:14:59.850
inclusive group of people, so drawing from
some of the faces you’d expect to see, who

01:14:59.850 --> 01:15:05.690
are leaders of organisations, leaders of systems,
but also having some new faces, people representing

01:15:05.690 --> 01:15:12.110
the voluntary sector, patients and carer voices
themselves, and front-line clinicians. And

01:15:12.110 --> 01:15:18.199
so, what we have tried to do is to try and
build an Assembly drawn from all of those

01:15:18.199 --> 01:15:23.230
constituencies. And you’ve seen the results
that have been circulated to you, but it comes

01:15:23.230 --> 01:15:30.599
with eight clinical leaders, 11 front-line
staff, ten health and care system leaders,

01:15:30.599 --> 01:15:36.849
nine from the voluntary sector, ten from a
patient carer and public voice background,

01:15:36.849 --> 01:15:40.889
and eight others. And recognising obviously
that people wear a number of different hats,

01:15:40.889 --> 01:15:45.690
but it is diverse in the way that the Board
wanted.

01:15:45.690 --> 01:15:48.850
It does have a number of prominent names,
so there will be chief executives and professors

01:15:48.850 --> 01:15:54.010
that you’ll recognise, but it also has lots
of new faces. I am particularly pleased that

01:15:54.010 --> 01:15:58.350
there’s a member of the – or an ex-member
of the NHS Youth Forum, who’s now a third-year

01:15:58.350 --> 01:16:04.380
medical student, who would be a member of
the Assembly; she is fantastic. An active

01:16:04.380 --> 01:16:10.460
participant at the Nuffield Health Summit
last month. There’s a neo-natal intensive

01:16:10.460 --> 01:16:16.980
care nurse, a paramedic, a midwife, a radiographer,
occupational therapist, NHS chaplain, as well

01:16:16.980 --> 01:16:24.020
as GPs and doctors. There are at least two
people with their own individual health budget,

01:16:24.020 --> 01:16:30.190
as well as parents, carers for young people
who also have health budgets, disability advocates

01:16:30.190 --> 01:16:34.820
and carers, as well as prominent commentators.
So, I think it’s a really diverse group

01:16:34.820 --> 01:16:40.770
of people and I think it will be a really
stimulating conversation. In terms of the

01:16:40.770 --> 01:16:47.920
diversity, it’s 53% female; we have people
from their 20s to their 70s; we have a pretty

01:16:47.920 --> 01:16:52.429
good geographical coverage; and we have a
quarter of the membership which would be BME.

01:16:52.429 --> 01:16:57.880
So, I think it’s been a really good process,
it’s still been very hard to say no to lots

01:16:57.880 --> 01:17:03.010
of fantastic people, but bearing in mind that
people also have a variety of different terms,

01:17:03.010 --> 01:17:07.619
there will be opportunities in the future
to join the group. So I think it does build

01:17:07.619 --> 01:17:13.800
on the lessons of Victor Adebowale’s taskforce.
It has a group of new faces, and I think we

01:17:13.800 --> 01:17:16.290
need to think about how we’re going to make
it work and how we’re going to make it do

01:17:16.290 --> 01:17:21.309
the job that it needs to do.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: [Inaudible]. I mean really

01:17:21.309 --> 01:17:24.260
fantastic. Ruth, did you want to say – add
anything?

01:17:24.260 --> 01:17:28.280
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: No, we’ve just been working
over the last couple of weeks to make sure

01:17:28.280 --> 01:17:35.360
that we have a really diverse and fresh faces
and names to it. So, well done to Neil and

01:17:35.360 --> 01:17:42.449
the team for getting that.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any…? No? I just think it’s

01:17:42.449 --> 01:17:49.570
just a really diverse, interesting, feisty
group, so we look forward to working with

01:17:49.570 --> 01:17:50.570
them.
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Indeed.

01:17:50.570 --> 01:17:53.260
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, well done Neil. It’s
a really great effort. A great result.

01:17:53.260 --> 01:17:58.290
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Thank you. If it’s as
feisty as the Youth Forum, I think it will

01:17:58.290 --> 01:18:02.079
be special.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And your next item as well,

01:18:02.079 --> 01:18:04.940
for you – number 9: the Empowering People
and Communities Taskforce.

01:18:04.940 --> 01:18:06.179
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Yes.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I think Michelle was going to [inaudible].

01:18:06.179 --> 01:18:10.699
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: No, I’m very happy
to. Co-chaired with Victor, of whom we would

01:18:10.699 --> 01:18:21.900
like to thank for his outstanding contribution.
The taskforce members – nice to see two

01:18:21.900 --> 01:18:28.960
I think, isn’t it Neil, who are on the Assembly.
A very good job they did too. And also, many

01:18:28.960 --> 01:18:34.920
of the citizens and carers who participated
in the programme, which was a taskforce over

01:18:34.920 --> 01:18:39.420
12 months with quite a specific and focused
remit.

01:18:39.420 --> 01:18:48.460
A couple of insights we would want to share.
Firstly, I think we felt and do feel very

01:18:48.460 --> 01:18:55.929
supportive that the Assembly is designed.
We have a number of fantastic names who are

01:18:55.929 --> 01:19:03.470
going onto it, and the taskforce was very,
very supportive and thoughtful in making recommendations

01:19:03.470 --> 01:19:13.440
about the design and the delivery of the Assembly.
I think we also felt that the way in which

01:19:13.440 --> 01:19:20.490
we work was quite important, reaching out
to a broad range of stakeholders, citizens,

01:19:20.490 --> 01:19:29.150
carers, as well as the voluntary sector, and
felt that it gave a good conduit for those

01:19:29.150 --> 01:19:36.340
people to feed in their thoughts and ideas
about what needed to change in the long-term

01:19:36.340 --> 01:19:39.750
plan.
So, one of the strongest themes that came

01:19:39.750 --> 01:19:48.770
out of the 12 months of work was a desire
to see a better focus on health inequalities,

01:19:48.770 --> 01:19:55.730
and that was great to see in the long-term
plan with that being picked up. I think the

01:19:55.730 --> 01:20:02.590
second thing was really making sure that in
the implementation of the long-term plan,

01:20:02.590 --> 01:20:08.750
that we continue to engage a breadth and depth
of communities, individuals and organisations,

01:20:08.750 --> 01:20:15.040
and continue the spirit of co-production.
So, good to see that that’s continuing to

01:20:15.040 --> 01:20:20.230
be a part of not only the Assembly but the
development of the implementation plan and

01:20:20.230 --> 01:20:29.070
framework over the forthcoming months. I think
we also felt that it was important to engage

01:20:29.070 --> 01:20:35.760
people in different methods, and social media
was an important part of that taskforce and

01:20:35.760 --> 01:20:41.739
it enabled us to reach out relatively cheaply
to large numbers of people who I think felt

01:20:41.739 --> 01:20:49.760
they had their voice heard.
So, in terms of next steps and perhaps legacy

01:20:49.760 --> 01:20:59.340
of the taskforce, much of the work is passed
over to the Assembly. I think absolutely welcome

01:20:59.340 --> 01:21:09.300
the voluntary sector and patient partners
on the Assembly. I think, as we talked about,

01:21:09.300 --> 01:21:21.139
really looking at two or three areas in terms
of our achievements, one of which is particularly

01:21:21.139 --> 01:21:26.409
continuing to ensure co-production is a big
part of what we do, continuing to make sure

01:21:26.409 --> 01:21:33.719
that the national programmes build on their
commitment to have specific objectives around

01:21:33.719 --> 01:21:38.320
inequality and targets, and looking to see
them being developed over the forthcoming

01:21:38.320 --> 01:21:47.869
period. And just welcome the ongoing focus
which runs right through the plan on the commitment

01:21:47.869 --> 01:21:53.800
to health inequalities and continuing to engage
citizens and the voluntary sector.

01:21:53.800 --> 01:21:58.100
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Michelle.
Ruth, could you – or Neil, do you want to

01:21:58.110 --> 01:21:59.900
add to that?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Nothing else to add. Thank you.

01:21:59.900 --> 01:22:05.179
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No? Anyone got any questions
for Michelle or…? Could you thank Sara Geater

01:22:05.179 --> 01:22:09.810
very much, Neil, on behalf of all of us, for
organising the NHS Assembly so well? I know

01:22:09.810 --> 01:22:12.530
she worked very closely with you on this?
Could you just say –

01:22:12.530 --> 01:22:15.389
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Certainly will.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: – just say she’s done a

01:22:15.389 --> 01:22:19.599
great job. Michelle, thank you very much for
that. Is that finished now, that taskforce?

01:22:19.599 --> 01:22:21.530
Yeah? Great.
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Yes.

01:22:21.530 --> 01:22:27.909
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Well, it’s borne fruit.
Excellent. Thank you very much. So, Emily,

01:22:27.909 --> 01:22:32.460
the Joint Standards of Business Conduct.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yes. We’re asking the Board

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:37.949
to ratify the policy which is attached to
the paper, which will now cover colleagues

01:22:37.949 --> 01:22:46.210
in both NHS England and NHS Improvement. There
was a difference in the two policies before

01:22:46.210 --> 01:22:52.469
we started, which largely stems from a different
definition of what a responsible person is,

01:22:52.469 --> 01:22:59.489
in the two organisations. We discussed this
at the Executive at the beginning of the month,

01:22:59.489 --> 01:23:03.520
and agreed that we should adopt broadly the
policy that already existed in NHS England,

01:23:03.520 --> 01:23:08.139
which had quite a broad definition of who
a responsible person was. And what that means

01:23:08.139 --> 01:23:15.150
is that any of our staff who are at Band 8d
and above will now need to make declarations

01:23:15.150 --> 01:23:19.940
of any potential conflicts of interests. Obviously,
the platform that’s already available for

01:23:19.940 --> 01:23:23.870
NHS England colleagues to do this will be
made available obviously to NHS Improvement

01:23:23.870 --> 01:23:28.370
colleagues as well, to make that easy to do.
And it’s in the interest of transparency

01:23:28.370 --> 01:23:33.469
and making sure every decision that’s made,
we’re completely transparent on both who’s

01:23:33.469 --> 01:23:35.940
been involved and what any potential issues
might have been.

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:40.010
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Emily. Can we approve
that please? Oh, sorry.

01:23:40.010 --> 01:23:42.810
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Can I just ask a question?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes, do, yeah.

01:23:42.810 --> 01:23:45.840
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: So, sort of maybe wearing an
Audit and Risk Commission hat –

01:23:45.840 --> 01:23:47.670
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yes.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: – Emily, I kind of looked

01:23:47.670 --> 01:23:53.010
at that quite carefully and I thought it looked
great. Two just sort of small comments reflecting

01:23:53.010 --> 01:23:58.090
on a couple of the practical challenges that
we in NHS England have come across in the

01:23:58.090 --> 01:24:03.610
last sort of number of months, and thinking
about how this could have helped with that.

01:24:03.610 --> 01:24:07.310
So, it references the social media policies
of both organisations.

01:24:07.310 --> 01:24:09.550
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I couldn’t find the NHS Improvement

01:24:09.550 --> 01:24:13.760
one, but I looked – I reminded myself, looked
at the NHS England one, which I think is very

01:24:13.760 --> 01:24:18.349
good, actually. I think it might be helpful
to add that in the list of related resources

01:24:18.349 --> 01:24:19.889
at the back of the document.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Okay. Yeah.

01:24:19.889 --> 01:24:24.300
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: That was one thought. And the
second thing I was thinking about was the

01:24:24.300 --> 01:24:30.660
issue about, particularly at a time when,
as a result of transformation, there’s quite

01:24:30.660 --> 01:24:34.940
a lot of fluidity and people are moving in
and out of the organisation a bit – a bit

01:24:34.940 --> 01:24:41.829
more than normally – the issues about new
jobs and waiting periods and so on, which

01:24:41.829 --> 01:24:44.050
I think is not particularly covered in this.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Okay.

01:24:44.050 --> 01:24:50.420
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: And I just wanted to ask you,
how do we make sure that our staff understand

01:24:50.420 --> 01:24:56.849
what the rules are, applying to any other
employment they might take on, you know, subsequent

01:24:56.849 --> 01:25:00.860
to being in one of our organisations.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: You mean how any obligations

01:25:00.860 --> 01:25:03.789
in this policy would continue if they changed
roles, or…?

01:25:03.789 --> 01:25:07.400
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: No, I’m more thinking about
what it’s – whether there’s any gap

01:25:07.400 --> 01:25:09.970
required between this job and one that relates
–

01:25:09.970 --> 01:25:12.130
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Oh yes.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: – in a similar subject, or

01:25:12.130 --> 01:25:13.130
–
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Okay.

01:25:13.130 --> 01:25:16.530
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: – in anticipation of a move,
how that might affect what they’re doing

01:25:16.530 --> 01:25:18.110
here.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yeah, it’s a good point.

01:25:18.110 --> 01:25:21.329
We do have restrictions, obviously, with what
employment people can take on and what the

01:25:21.329 --> 01:25:25.920
gaps are, and those are available in our policies,
which are on our website, but we can again

01:25:25.920 --> 01:25:28.300
make a link perhaps to make that more –
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I think that might be helpful.

01:25:28.300 --> 01:25:29.350
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: – obvious.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: It’s important that people

01:25:29.350 --> 01:25:33.909
are, you know, well briefed on that now.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Absolutely.

01:25:33.909 --> 01:25:40.590
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Thank you very much.
Ruth, the Slavery and Human Trafficking Statement.

01:25:40.590 --> 01:25:47.940
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, chair. This
is a function[?] of NHS England, in line with

01:25:47.940 --> 01:25:54.079
the Modern Slavery Act, and it’s a paper
for us to, I hope, approve; a statement that

01:25:54.079 --> 01:26:00.119
Simon will sign. It sets out what we’ve
done and what we’re going to do. And Hilary

01:26:00.119 --> 01:26:03.990
Garrett, my deputy, has been leading this
and is a real expert nationally in this area.

01:26:03.990 --> 01:26:11.570
So, I have nothing else further to add, and
of course I will try and answer any questions

01:26:11.570 --> 01:26:16.170
if there are any.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Thank you very much.

01:26:16.170 --> 01:26:23.150
No? Right, thank you. Is Stephen Groves joining
us, or…? Yeah. So, the EPRR. Matthew or

01:26:23.150 --> 01:26:26.110
Stephen.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah. Well, let me just

01:26:26.110 --> 01:26:33.140
give a 30-second introduction while Stephen
makes himself comfortable and welcome. NHS

01:26:33.140 --> 01:26:39.260
England is statutorily a Category 1 responder
under EPRR, so it’s one of our statutory

01:26:39.260 --> 01:26:45.150
obligations which I hold as the board member
and Stephen does all the work on, which is

01:26:45.150 --> 01:26:50.590
the way these things are supposed to work.
So, I think from this board’s point of view,

01:26:50.590 --> 01:26:55.969
people will now understand that this is no
longer a theoretical risk. Just in the past

01:26:55.969 --> 01:27:03.800
year, the Salisbury novichok, the clinical
waste issue, the MERS and monkeypox, EpiPens,

01:27:03.800 --> 01:27:11.699
the collapse of a major domiciliary provider
have all happened, and within the time I’ve

01:27:11.699 --> 01:27:16.250
been here we’ve had two major terrorist
attacks in London, we’ve had the Manchester

01:27:16.250 --> 01:27:26.130
attack and we’ve had WannaCry.
So, we depend very heavily on the deep expertise

01:27:26.130 --> 01:27:32.900
of the EPRR team, and part of what we’ve
done quite recently is conduct an audit which

01:27:32.900 --> 01:27:36.900
has come back and said, ‘Look, stuff is
changing now. There are more incidents than

01:27:36.900 --> 01:27:41.739
there’ve ever been before. They now overlap
and, quite importantly, the nature of them

01:27:41.739 --> 01:27:46.940
changes.’ The NHS is very good when a crisis
happens and 48&nbsp;hours it’s all over, but

01:27:46.940 --> 01:27:50.949
we now have incidents that run on for weeks
and weeks and weeks, and other ones layer

01:27:50.949 --> 01:27:58.469
on top of them, and the team that Stephen
leads never leaves their post. They’re not

01:27:58.469 --> 01:28:05.140
personally responsible for the EU exit programme,
but the expertise they have is the basis on

01:28:05.140 --> 01:28:09.560
which we’ve built the whole of the EU exit
preparation programmes through the NHS, because

01:28:09.560 --> 01:28:14.270
the NHS knows that this is best practice.
So, Stephen, thank you for coming and walk

01:28:14.270 --> 01:28:17.160
us through the paper, and I’m sure people
will have interesting questions.

01:28:17.160 --> 01:28:22.679
&gt;&gt;Stephen Groves: Thank you, Matthew. As Matthew
says, we have a number of duties under the

01:28:22.679 --> 01:28:28.870
Civil Contingencies Act as a Category 1 responder,
but also under the NHS Act. And one of those

01:28:28.870 --> 01:28:34.829
requirements is that we bring a paper to the
Board each and every year to talk about our

01:28:34.829 --> 01:28:40.540
responsibilities and what we’re doing to
meet those responsibilities. NHS England has

01:28:40.540 --> 01:28:46.829
an additional responsibility that we are required
to make sure that we, NHS England, but also

01:28:46.829 --> 01:28:53.420
the NHS in England, is properly prepared to
deal with the range of threats. And I think

01:28:53.420 --> 01:28:58.320
Matthew has covered a number of the threats
and hazards that we deal with.

01:28:58.320 --> 01:29:05.820
So, since Matthew brought a paper this time
last year, my team and I have been involved

01:29:05.820 --> 01:29:13.610
in managing 65 incidents, ten of them which
required some significant national coordination

01:29:13.610 --> 01:29:19.599
and five of them occurring consecutively.
As Matthew alluded to, we were involved in

01:29:19.599 --> 01:29:26.699
the coordination of the novichok and the other
incident in Salisbury, in Amesbury, and that

01:29:26.699 --> 01:29:33.159
was quite a protracted incident, and dealing
very sensitively with counter-terrorism policing

01:29:33.159 --> 01:29:39.059
and other partners and managing the consequence
of that very closely with Public Health England

01:29:39.059 --> 01:29:45.900
and the Department of Health, along to things
like the disruption of a clinical waste provider.

01:29:45.900 --> 01:29:51.260
And clearly, it’s extremely important that
our NHS organisations can dispose of clinical

01:29:51.260 --> 01:29:57.960
waste and get them offsite as quickly as possible,
so quite significant disruption to hospitals

01:29:57.960 --> 01:30:02.340
was a potential if this had been allowed to
continue.

01:30:02.340 --> 01:30:09.190
In September last year, we saw a case of MERS,
Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome, in the

01:30:09.190 --> 01:30:16.750
country, which was very quickly followed by
two unrelated cases of monkeypox, the first

01:30:16.750 --> 01:30:21.489
time in the UK that we’ve ever seen cases
of monkeypox, and this allowed us to test

01:30:21.489 --> 01:30:27.489
our high-consequence infectious disease arrangements.
And I’m pleased to say they worked very

01:30:27.489 --> 01:30:35.410
well. Again, dealing with a shortage of EpiPen.
There were incidents where we had a number

01:30:35.410 --> 01:30:43.980
of malicious parcels sent to NHS organisations
and academic institutions; and again, working

01:30:43.980 --> 01:30:48.790
very closely with Public Health England and
counter-terrorism policing to make sure that

01:30:48.790 --> 01:30:55.619
a) the product that was being sent around
wasn’t harmful, but also making sure that

01:30:55.619 --> 01:31:01.639
we were communicating with organisations around
what they should do if they were to receive

01:31:01.639 --> 01:31:07.280
such a parcel and dealing with those.
We’ve been dealing with things like the

01:31:07.280 --> 01:31:13.950
collapse of a domiciliary care provider, and
clearly significant facilities management

01:31:13.950 --> 01:31:19.570
providers either going into administration
or pre-pack administration. So, making sure

01:31:19.570 --> 01:31:27.150
that we have oversight of arrangements such
as that, but also that the NHS organisations

01:31:27.150 --> 01:31:32.719
have plans in place to deal with that and
to mitigate against the impact of those, and

01:31:32.719 --> 01:31:38.320
clearly continue to provide safe and effective
patient care. And that’s most important.

01:31:38.320 --> 01:31:43.480
So, part of our oversight is to make sure
the organisations are doing that, and we do

01:31:43.480 --> 01:31:51.039
that by undertaking an annual assurance exercise,
so each and every NHS provider organisation

01:31:51.039 --> 01:31:57.300
takes a statement to a board meeting such
as this to look and say how they are meeting

01:31:57.300 --> 01:32:02.780
the requirements of the core standards, and
they detail exactly what’s required of those

01:32:02.780 --> 01:32:09.610
organisations. We review that at a local,
regional and national level, and we indeed

01:32:09.610 --> 01:32:15.550
do the same exercise and we’re peer reviewed
by an organisation. And this year, we were

01:32:15.550 --> 01:32:21.520
peer reviewed by NHS Improvement as part of
that exercise. I’m pleased to say that the

01:32:21.520 --> 01:32:28.000
results of the assurance give substantial
compliance against the EPRR core standards,

01:32:28.000 --> 01:32:34.480
but also allow us to build on that to develop
a work programme for next year.

01:32:34.480 --> 01:32:39.449
The other part is around the development of
guidance for the NHS, and I’m really pleased

01:32:39.449 --> 01:32:45.030
this year, in consultation with Surgeon General,
Ministry of Defence and clinical colleagues,

01:32:45.030 --> 01:32:51.380
that we were able to produce and publish some
clinical guidelines for dealing with mass

01:32:51.380 --> 01:32:58.550
casualties. A lot of the expertise came from
the Ministry of Defence and their dealing

01:32:58.550 --> 01:33:04.969
with events overseas and during battle, and
therefore we’ve drawn on that and this is

01:33:04.969 --> 01:33:10.840
now available to all NHS organisations, and
indeed on the NHS England website, for use

01:33:10.840 --> 01:33:17.039
should we come across catastrophic injuries,
the type of which MoD have the experience

01:33:17.039 --> 01:33:21.409
in dealing with. So, I’m delighted that
we were able to produce that this year. And

01:33:21.409 --> 01:33:27.429
the other parts are around other guidelines
for dealing with the arrangements for reception

01:33:27.429 --> 01:33:31.329
of mass casualties and how we work with our
trauma centres.

01:33:31.329 --> 01:33:36.440
So, I’m really pleased that the work that
we’ve continued, the excellent team that

01:33:36.440 --> 01:33:41.880
I’m working with, have been able to deal
with these incidents, to provide oversight

01:33:41.880 --> 01:33:46.830
and to provide assurance to Matthew and to
you, the Board, in relation to our arrangements

01:33:46.830 --> 01:33:52.260
that are in place.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Stephen.

01:33:52.260 --> 01:33:57.980
Any questions for Stephen? Tim.
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: I’m interested in – and

01:33:57.980 --> 01:34:09.400
you know that the Board and the NHS more broadly
is interested in the inter-connectedness of

01:34:09.400 --> 01:34:15.219
the health information systems, in order to
deliver more coordinated care, and with that

01:34:15.219 --> 01:34:21.290
greater interconnectedness comes increased
cyber-security risk. So that’s – I’m

01:34:21.290 --> 01:34:26.520
just interested about your – sort of as
that ramps up over the coming years, as I

01:34:26.520 --> 01:34:35.170
expect everyone wants it to, is the attendant
oversight and contingency planning also keeping

01:34:35.170 --> 01:34:38.530
pace with that?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Groves: Indeed. So, we responded

01:34:38.530 --> 01:34:44.389
to the WannaCry incident last year, and I
think we learned an awful lot from that. We’ve

01:34:44.389 --> 01:34:50.350
put in place arrangements for how we respond
to cyber-security. We clearly work very closely

01:34:50.350 --> 01:34:55.789
with NHS Digital, who provide oversight. And
I think one of the major things that we learned

01:34:55.789 --> 01:35:01.789
were around monitoring where organisations
are applying patches when they are available

01:35:01.789 --> 01:35:09.820
for issues which work for them. So, most definitely,
I think it’s extremely important. We’ve

01:35:09.820 --> 01:35:15.179
done a number of exercises in relation to
how we might respond to a cyber-incident because

01:35:15.179 --> 01:35:20.460
of its potential impact on patients and patient
care, so it’s extremely important to us

01:35:20.460 --> 01:35:22.659
and I think something we’ll continue to
build on.

01:35:22.659 --> 01:35:28.559
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think the – we did
learn a lot from the WannaCry incident, not

01:35:28.559 --> 01:35:35.119
least how do you communicate with your people
when the communication systems go down. And

01:35:35.119 --> 01:35:40.269
so we’ve developed new ways of ensuring
that we’re not thrown if email gets turned

01:35:40.269 --> 01:35:48.119
off, simple stuff like that. But the huge
advantage of the EPRR approach that we have

01:35:48.119 --> 01:35:56.269
is that when that incident happened, I called
the incidents within probably an hour of it

01:35:56.269 --> 01:36:00.510
starting to unravel, and at that moment there
was no question what the command and control

01:36:00.510 --> 01:36:04.619
was, there was no debating about who’s in
charge and who carries it, and – there was

01:36:04.619 --> 01:36:09.539
clarity instantly in place about where decisions
would be made and who could call on resources

01:36:09.539 --> 01:36:13.489
and so on. And that was the first time I’d
been right at the centre of one of these,

01:36:13.489 --> 01:36:17.989
and it is extraordinary when you watch it
work, how well it works.

01:36:17.989 --> 01:36:22.250
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Well, we’re unaware
of what you do most of the time, which is

01:36:22.250 --> 01:36:24.960
fortunate really. Thank you very much for
the report. Thank you.

01:36:24.960 --> 01:36:28.400
&gt;&gt;Stephen Groves: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Matt, anything – use of the

01:36:28.400 --> 01:36:30.909
Seal?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Nothing in particular to

01:36:30.909 --> 01:36:35.590
draw your attention to. I’m grateful to
Lesley Tillotson, who’s worked very closely

01:36:35.590 --> 01:36:40.510
with me as custodian of the Seal and has done
that with great care and diligence.

01:36:40.510 --> 01:36:44.289
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. And Lesley, this
is – Lesley’s sitting over there. This

01:36:44.289 --> 01:36:49.150
is your last board meeting, I think, Lesley.
Can I just thank you very much for all that

01:36:49.150 --> 01:36:52.300
you’ve done? And you’ve been brilliant,
actually. You’ve helped – your notes really

01:36:52.300 --> 01:36:56.829
helped me a lot.
Just before we finish off, I mean Stephen

01:36:56.829 --> 01:37:00.829
and Kathy have been – I don’t know how
long you’ve both worked for the NHS, but

01:37:00.829 --> 01:37:06.159
you’ve worked for NHS Improvement and before
that with Monitor for a long time. I mean…

01:37:06.159 --> 01:37:09.929
&gt;&gt;Kathy McLean: And the TDA.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And the TDA in your case, Kathy.

01:37:09.929 --> 01:37:12.400
If you’d like – any reflections you’d
like to sort of leave us with for a couple

01:37:12.400 --> 01:37:23.199
of minutes, at the end of the meeting?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Hay: I think it’s been a privilege,

01:37:23.199 --> 01:37:31.050
actually. I’ve worked in the NHS since December
2003, through the whole Foundation Trust regime,

01:37:31.050 --> 01:37:33.659
Monitor, into NHS Improvement, and I’ve
thoroughly enjoyed it. The thing that has

01:37:33.659 --> 01:37:39.909
always struck me is the dedication of the
staff in the NHS to care for the patients,

01:37:39.909 --> 01:37:47.820
and all I’d like to do is to wish you and
NHSI and NHSE all the best for the future

01:37:47.820 --> 01:37:53.469
and delivering the ten-year plan.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Kathy?

01:37:53.469 --> 01:38:00.019
&gt;&gt;Kathy McLean: Yes, thank you. I’ve been
in the NHS almost 36 years, so it’s a long

01:38:00.019 --> 01:38:06.840
time, and I’ve obviously seen it from absolutely
the front line all the way through. I’ve

01:38:06.840 --> 01:38:13.389
been very, very privileged to work at every
level in the NHS. And like Stephen, I think

01:38:13.389 --> 01:38:20.000
that my sense is that the one-point-whatever,
1.4&nbsp;million people or whatever are incredibly

01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:26.710
dedicated, and I think we are remarkably lucky
to have such a dedicated staff. And I think

01:38:26.710 --> 01:38:32.570
as I reflect, I look back and I think, ‘If
only I’d known what I know now back at the

01:38:32.570 --> 01:38:36.030
beginning.’ And I think there’s a constant
learning, isn’t there? And I would just

01:38:36.030 --> 01:38:40.429
urge you to – I mean, I wish you all the
luck with all of this. I think it’s a really

01:38:40.429 --> 01:38:48.130
important point of change in approach, and
the sort of joining up at every level feels

01:38:48.130 --> 01:38:54.400
absolutely the right thing to do and I really,
really support it. But I think if you keep

01:38:54.400 --> 01:39:00.400
in mind, as Stephen says: you know, we’re
here purely for the patients and the population,

01:39:00.400 --> 01:39:05.533
and encourage the staff – release them in
order to be great, because they will. They

01:39:05.533 --> 01:39:12.320
will innovate and they will make those changes.
And help them be happy, as we talked about

01:39:12.320 --> 01:39:16.353
earlier. So, all the best with all of that.
And we’re not quite gone yet, are we, but

01:39:16.353 --> 01:39:17.353
we’re on our way, we’re on our way. Thank
you.

01:39:17.353 --> 01:39:23.679
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, you both embody the best
values of the NHS, so on behalf of us all,

01:39:23.679 --> 01:39:27.260
you know, thank you both very much. And look,
you’re not going to escape forever. You

01:39:27.260 --> 01:39:32.260
know very well that you’ll be back somehow
in the NHS before too long, I’m sure, in

01:39:32.260 --> 01:39:36.719
some way. Good. Any other business from anyone?
Good, thank you.

